INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2575,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10600715','Patient Perspectives on Social Risk Screening and Documentation in a Dermatology Clinic.','Wilkerson K, De Marchis E, Rudd N, Williams JC, Pantell M, Ackerman SL, Amerson EH, Chang AY.','2023','cantonese touchpoint stigmatizing srqr cantonese flyer ahc cantonese qumu critiqued ahc ahc genderqueer cantonese genderqueer cantonese houseless shut chicano rican filipino guamanian chamorro samoan genderqueer cantonese houseless shut chicano rican filipino guamanian chamorro samoan genderqueer cantonese houseless shut chicano rican filipino guamanian chamorro samoan 40s 60s 40s 40s 60s 60s divulge anybody anybody 40s indifferent weren 40s adamant 60s transcends neurotic 40s 60s demeanor insecure demeaning diverged defiant throws touchpoint cracks ashamed unhoused rinse ointments subsidize mutuality antidiscrimination cantonese penman talih moonesinghe bouye beckles s42 braveman egerter socialdeterminants alderwick ahcm policysearch policyfinder 20access 20to 20screening 20tools 20social 20determinants 20health 2fsocial 20determinants 20health 20in 20payment 20models 20h 2famadoc 2fhod a19 desilvey fichtenberg bernheim peltz healthaffairs marchis aceves siren sirenetwork s10900 mesia cwalina levoska mostaghimi laursen ljad040 amerson amerson lowes barilla strunk lentiginous mostaghimi noe jdin honig cronholm shabazz mcklaine e23 ajmc kalow ciccolo barnidge krupsky labarge s10995 palakshappa hpu marchis hessler fichtenberg s25 s37 pikcilingis jadohealth fleegler muret wagstaff e1332 e1341 wylie krull jtt selvaraj aschkenasy hamity schickedanz colvin bettenhausen collie akers butz dworkin serwint fetters guetterman fetters worksheets ahcm screeningtool billioux verlander alley broaddus fife jantz reno nederveld e3075 e3085 e0282103 mccracken adepoju liaw e2239855 anuforo byhoff marchis hessler s38 s46 kingstone s11135 1478088706qp063oa diloreto gaines ijlter ijlter samhsa samhsa samhsa priv sma14','This mixed-methods study assesses whether dermatology clinics are acceptable settings for patient disclosures of adverse social determinants of health. Key Points Question What are patients’ perspectives on social risk screening and documentation in a dermatology clinic? Findings In this mixed-methods study of 135 survey and interview participants, 116 rated social risk screening as very or somewhat appropriate and 85 were completely or somewhat comfortable with documentation of social risks in the electronic health records. Interviewees shared that interpersonal factors and institutional trust were factors in their willingness to discuss social risks. Meaning Findings of this study were similar to findings in the primary care setting and suggest that optimizing patients’ trust in their physicians and the medical system, while addressing privacy and discrimination concerns, may help facilitate disclosure of social risks. Abstract Importance Providing person-centered dermatologic care includes consideration of social risk factors, such as housing instability and unreliable transportation, that may affect clinical management. Patients’ perspectives on social risk screening and documentation in dermatology clinics have not yet been evaluated. Objective To understand patients’ perspectives on social risk screening and documentation in a dermatology clinic. Design, Setting, and Participants This mixed-methods study used a survey and semistructured interviews and was conducted in a general dermatology clinic at a large urban public hospital. Patients at the clinic were eligible to complete the survey if they were 18 years or older; able to speak and read English, Spanish, or Cantonese; and comfortable using a computer tablet. Survey participants who preferred to use English were eligible for interviews. The survey included social risk screening questions, measures of acceptability, and questions on social risk factors associated with patient acceptability. Semistructured interviews were conducted to explore attitudes and beliefs about social risk screening and documentation. Survey and interview findings were integrated during data analysis through development of themes and joint display. Data were analyzed from December 2021 to April 2023. Main Outcomes and Measures There were 2 outcome measures of acceptability: appropriateness of screening in a dermatology clinic and comfort with documentation of social risk in the electronic health record (EHR). Results A total of 135 participants (including 73 males [54.1%]) answered both measures of acceptability in the survey. Of these participants, 116 (85.9%) reported that social risk screening in their dermatology clinic was very or somewhat appropriate and 85 (63.0%) reported being completely or somewhat comfortable with having their social risks documented in the EHR. Themes that were developed from surveys and interviews were the (1) role of interpersonal factors in willingness to disclose social risks, (2) implications of institutional trust for willingness to disclose and comfort with documentation, and (3) relevance of screening in a dermatology clinic. Conclusions and Relevance Results of this study showed that most participants found social risk screening to be appropriate in a dermatology clinic, although a smaller proportion of participants were comfortable with EHR documentation of their social risks. Optimizing patients’ trust in their physicians and the medical system, while addressing privacy and discrimination concerns, may help facilitate disclosure of social risks. Introduction The health and well-being of individuals and communities are affected by social determinants of health, including housing, transportation, and educational level. 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 In this mixed-methods study, we referred to social determinants of health that adversely affect patients as social risks or social risk factors. 5 National organizations endorse screening for social risks in clinical settings, 6 , 7 , 8 , 9 and screening is increasingly associated with reimbursement and accreditation. 10 Most studies examining social risk screening have been conducted in primary care and emergency department (ED) settings. 11 Yet consideration of social risk factors is a component of providing person-centered dermatologic care. Transportation challenges, including unreliable transportation and distance to care, affect health care access 12 and are factors in accessing dermatologic care. 13 , 14 Housing status and housing quality are associated with dermatologic diagnoses and affect clinical decision-making. 15 , 16 , 17 , 18 Furthermore, lower socioeconomic status (SES) is associated with increased severity of chronic inflammatory skin conditions, including atopic dermatitis and hidradenitis suppurativa, 16 , 19 and poor melanoma survival. 20 , 21 , 22 Given that patients with lower SES are likely to have at least 1 social risk, 16 , 19 , 20 , 21 , 22 lower SES is associated with poor dermatologic disease outcomes, 16 , 19 , 20 , 21 , 22 and dermatologic care may be the only touchpoint with the health care system for some patients with chronic skin diseases, 23 it is reasonable to consider whether dermatology clinics are appropriate settings for social risk screening and related interventions. Although social risk data can inform the diagnosis and management of dermatologic conditions, 17 , 18 social risk screening involves the disclosure of private and potentially stigmatizing information. 24 , 25 , 26 , 27 Multiple studies support patient acceptability of screening in primary care and the ED, 28 , 29 , 30 , 31 , 32 , 33 , 34 , 35 but patient acceptability of screening and documentation in dermatology clinics has not yet been evaluated. To address this knowledge gap, we conducted this mixed-methods study with the aim of understanding patients’ perspectives on social risk screening and documentation in a dermatology clinic. Methods We performed a convergent mixed-methods study with a convenience sample of adult patients in a general dermatology clinic at a large urban public hospital. The University of California, San Francisco Institutional Review Board approved this study. All participants provided written informed consent. We followed the Strengthening the Reporting of Observational Studies in Epidemiology ( STROBE ) and Standards for Reporting Qualitative Research ( SRQR ) reporting guidelines. The quantitative component (a survey) and the qualitative component (semistructured interviews) of the study were collected and analyzed in parallel and then merged ( Figure ). We integrated data through purposive sampling, narrative text, and joint display. 36 , 37 Figure. Visual Model for Convergent Mixed-Methods Study Design. Open in a new tab Study Population Study participants were recruited from June to October 2021. Eligibility for survey participation included age 18 years or older; ability to provide informed consent; ability to speak and read English, Spanish, or Cantonese; no immediate medical attention required; and comfort with using a computer tablet. At appointment check-in, clinic staff shared a study recruitment flyer with all patients. Eligibility screening and the informed consent process were conducted in a private room by study personnel. Telephone interpreters were provided to patients who preferred to use a non-English language. After survey completion, those who preferred to use English were purposively sampled for interview participation. Quantitative Component: Procedures, Measures, and Outcomes Participants completed the survey on a computer tablet before, during, or after their clinic visit. The survey consisted of the 10-item Accountable Health Communities (AHC) health-related social needs screening tool, 38 , 39 which is widely used 28 , 40 , 41 , 42 , 43 , 44 and includes questions on housing, food, transportation, utilities, and interpersonal violence; 2 outcome measures of acceptability; and 20 questions on social risk factors associated with patient acceptability (eAppendix in Supplement 1 ). We modified questions from an earlier study evaluating acceptability of social risk screening in primary care clinics and EDs 28 , 45 for use in a dermatology clinic setting. Study materials in English were professionally translated into Spanish and Chinese. Survey data were collected and managed using Research Electronic Data Capture tools. Survey participants received $10 as compensation for their time. We used 2 outcome measures of acceptability. The first was appropriateness of screening in the participants’ dermatology clinic: “Do you think it is appropriate to be asked these questions about your social and economic needs at this dermatology clinic?” The second was comfort with documentation of social risks in the electronic health record (EHR): “Would you be comfortable having your responses to these questions included in your health records (also known as your medical record or chart)?” For each survey question, participants could select the answer that fit their situation. For questions regarding the 2 measures of acceptability, participants chose responses on a 5-point Likert scale ranging from very appropriate or completely comfortable on one end to very inappropriate or completely uncomfortable on the other end, with a midpoint of neither appropriate nor inappropriate or neither comfortable nor uncomfortable. Qualitative Component: Sampling and Procedures Following survey completion, we invited participants who preferred English to participate in 20-minute semistructured interviews. We initially used convenience sampling. After 31 surveys and 5 interviews, it became apparent that most participants were accepting of social risk screening. We then transitioned to purposive sampling of participants who were not accepting of social risk screening to ensure that the perspectives of these participants were also represented. Due to resource limitations, we did not conduct interviews with participants who preferred speaking Spanish or Cantonese. Interviewees received $25 as compensation for their time. Telephone interviews were audio-recorded using videoconferencing software (Zoom; Qumu Corp) and were professionally transcribed verbatim. Transcripts were deidentified and stored in a HIPAA (Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act)-compliant, secure electronic folder (Box; Box Inc). Two of us (K.W. and A.Y.C.) periodically discussed ideas and concepts captured in these interviews. We continued interviews until data saturation was reached (ie, no new ideas and concepts could be identified). 46 Data Analysis For quantitative data, we dichotomized the survey responses to the acceptability outcome measures (appropriateness of screening and comfort with EHR documentation) into appropriate or comfortable (representing the responses very or somewhat appropriate and very or somewhat comfortable) and not appropriate or not comfortable (representing the responses neither, somewhat, or very inappropriate and neither, somewhat, or very uncomfortable). We used χ 2 and Fisher exact tests to identify associations (indicated by 2-sided P < .05) between the 2 acceptability measures and participant characteristics. Analysis was performed with Stata 17 SE (StataCorp LLC). For qualitative data, 2 of us (K.W. and N.R.) initially coded a small subset of transcripts independently to generate codes and then met iteratively to discuss discrepancies in codes to reach consensus as well as create a common coding style. After the codebook was finalized and a common coding style was agreed on, we (K.W. and N.R.) each served as primary coder on half of transcripts and as secondary coder on the other half. Another team member (A.Y.C.) adjudicated differences and facilitated discussions to reach consensus throughout the coding process. We used thematic analysis 47 to develop themes, which involved an inductive approach and constant comparative analysis of codes, coded text, field notes written after each interview, and reflective memos that captured emerging ideas during coding. Some of us with expertise in qualitative analysis (E.D.M. and S.L.A.) critiqued themes iteratively. We integrated quantitative and qualitative data during the development of themes by analyzing the qualitative data based on responses to the 2 outcome measures of acceptability and participant characteristics associated with acceptability in the quantitative analysis. We analyzed data from December 2021 to April 2023. Results A total of 185 adult patients were recruited for this study, of whom 35 declined to participate and 10 did not meet the inclusion criteria. Of the 140 patients who consented to participate and started the survey (for a 75.7% response rate), 135 (96.4%) answered both measures of acceptability questions and were included in the analyses (eFigure in Supplement 1 ). Among the 135 participants, 102 (75.6%) had at least 1 of 5 social risk factors based on the AHC survey cutoff points. One hundred twenty-eight (94.8%) did not skip or select “Prefer not to answer” for any of the AHC survey questions. 38 Those who completed surveys included 52 females (38.5%), 73 males (54.1%), and 8 individuals (5.9%) who identified as transgender male or man, transgender female or woman, genderqueer or gender nonconforming, or different identity. The language preference for most participants was English (107 [79.3%]), followed by Spanish (20 [14.8%]) and Cantonese (8 [5.9%]). Of the 135 participants, 116 (85.9%) reported that social risk screening in a dermatology clinic was appropriate (ie, answered very or somewhat appropriate) and 85 (63.0%) reported being comfortable (ie, answered completely or somewhat comfortable) with EHR documentation of their social risk. Screening appropriateness did not differ based on participant characteristics ( Table 1 ). A higher proportion of participants who were comfortable with documentation desired assistance with social risks compared with participants who were uncomfortable with documentation (61.2% [52 of 85] vs 38.0% [19 of 50]; P = .01) ( Table 2 ). A higher proportion of participants who rated screening as inappropriate were uncomfortable with documentation compared with participants who rated screening as appropriate (63.2% [12 of 19] vs 32.8% [38 of 116]; P = .01). Table 1. Participant Characteristics by Survey Response to Acceptability of Screening at This Dermatology Clinic. Characteristic Total No. (%) Appropriateness of screening, No. (%) a P value Not appropriate b Appropriate c All participants 135 (100) 19 (14.1) 116 (85.9) Age, y 18-44 40 (29.6) 6 (31.6) 34 (29.3) >.99 45-64 68 (50.4) 10 (52.6) 58 (50.0) ≥65 26 (19.3) 3 (15.8) 23 (19.8) Missing data 1 (0.7) 0 1 (0.9) Gender identity d Male 73 (54.1) 9 (47.4) 64 (55.2) .37 Female 52 (38.5) 6 (31.6) 46 (39.7) Transgender male or man 1 (0.7) 0 1 (0.9) Transgender female or woman 3 (2.2) 1 (5.3) 2 (1.7) Genderqueer or gender nonconforming 2 (1.5) 0 2 (1.7) Different identity 2 (1.5) 1 (5.3) 1 (0.9) Missing data 2 (1.5) 2 (10.5) 0 Race and ethnicity d Hispanic or Latino e 37 (27.4) 6 (31.6) 31 (26.7) .10 Non-Hispanic Asian f 13 (9.6) 1 (5.3) 12 (10.3) Non-Hispanic Black or African American 12 (8.9) 3 (15.6) 9 (7.8) Non-Hispanic White 49 (36.3) 3 (15.8) 46 (39.7) Other g 14 (10.4) 4 (21.1) 10 (8.6) Missing data 10 (7.4) 2 (10.5) 8 (6.9) Educational level, y <12 22 (16.3) 2 (10.5) 20 (17.2) .74 ≥12 112 (83.0) 17 (89.5) 95 (81.9) Missing data 1 (0.7) 0 1 (0.9) Preferred language English 107 (79.3) 15 (78.9) 92 (79.3) >.99 Spanish 20 (14.8) 3 (15.8) 17 (14.7) Cantonese 8 (5.9) 1 (5.3) 7 (6.0) Length of time receiving care at this dermatology clinic, y <1 60 (44.4) 12 (63.2) 48 (41.4) .56 1 to <3 24 (17.8) 3 (15.8) 21 (18.1) 3 to <5 18 (13.3) 1 (5.3) 17 (14.7) ≥5 29 (21.5) 3 (15.8) 26 (22.4) Missing data 4 (3.0) 0 4 (3.4) Self-reported health Excellent 15 (11.1) 3 (15.8) 12 (10.3) .88 Very good 26 (19.3) 4 (2