HR documentation of social risks Completely comfortable 63 (46.7) 53 (49.1) 10 (37.0) .63 Somewhat comfortable 22 (16.3) 17 (15.7) 5 (18.5) Neither comfortable nor uncomfortable 26 (19.3) 21 (19.4) 5 (18.5) Somewhat uncomfortable 20 (14.8) 14 (13.0) 6 (22.2) Completely uncomfortable 4 (3.0) 3 (2.8) 1 (3.7) Open in a new tab Abbreviation: EHR, electronic health record. a Gender identity and race and ethnicity were self-reported in the survey by participants. b Hispanic or Latino included individuals who identified as Cuban; other Hispanic, Latino, or Spanish origin; Mexican, Mexican American, and Chicano; and Puerto Rican. c Non-Hispanic Asian included individuals who did not identify as Hispanic or Latino but identified as Asian Indian, Chinese, Filipino, Japanese, Korean, Vietnamese, or other Asian. d Other included individuals who did not identify as Hispanic or Latino but identified with 2 or more races or one of the following categories: American Indian or Alaska Native, Native Hawaiian, Guamanian or Chamorro, Samoan, Other Pacific Islander, or some other race. e Indicates statistically significant ( P < .05). Three themes were developed and refined through analysis based on survey responses to the appropriateness of screening, comfort with EHR documentation, and desire for assistance: (1) role of interpersonal factors in willingness to disclose social risks, (2) implications of institutional trust for willingness to disclose and comfort with documentation, and (3) relevance of screening in a dermatology clinic ( Table 4 ). Within some themes, we identified patterns based on appropriateness of screening and/or comfort with EHR documentation. We did not identify patterns based on desire for assistance. Table 4. Joint Display of the Integration of Quantitative and Qualitative Data. Theme Pattern Quote from interviewees Appropriateness of screening Comfort with EHR documentation Role of interpersonal factors in willingness to disclose social risks NA From a woman aged mid-40s to mid-60s: “My relationship with her is...I trust her I feel comfortable talking with her; she was my mom’s doctor.…I’ve known her for a long time so I feel very comfortable talking to her about it.” Somewhat appropriate Somewhat comfortable NA From a woman aged late-teens to mid-40s: “And it’s a little like a feeling of inadequacy, right? And then also being in a dermatologist office, it’s also being in a setting amongst other people that are not in the same situation, you know. [It’s] also like an added layer.” Somewhat inappropriate Somewhat uncomfortable Implication of institutional trust for willingness to disclose and comfort with documentation Screening acceptability From a man aged mid-40s to mid-60s: “A doctor is a doctor to me. They’re there to help me.…Unless it’s proven otherwise, I’m going to assume what they’re asking me is for my own good.” Very appropriate Completely comfortable From a man aged mid-60s or older: “Because I don’t think I should be forced to divulge personal information to anybody: a doctor or anybody. I did it voluntarily. He would have to explain to me why that personal information would make his treatment for me better.” Neither appropriate nor inappropriate Completely comfortable Documentation acceptability From a man aged late-teens to mid-40s: “I felt indifferent about answering the questions. They weren’t too personal. And I trust that the hospital will keep the information safe and sound within my own records and within closed doors.” Somewhat appropriate Completely comfortable From a man aged late-teens to mid-40s: “[I’m] uncomfortable because it could change. So if I was homeless at one point and then maybe not homeless in a year or two years, I’d be very, very adamant to try and go into the hospital if I was aware that, say, my situation changed. I have a home now and I’m proud of my home, and I have a job and am good again and I’m stable.” Very appropriate Somewhat uncomfortable Relevance of screening in a dermatology clinic NA From a nonbinary person aged mid-60s or older: “And, you know, my attitude is like, look, you want to gather all the best information you can and you’re being asked to contribute to that. And so that transcends whatever like little personal neurotic aspects you might have, at least that’s how I think of it. It’s like, look, you’re contributing to something that’s going to make it better for everybody.” Neither appropriate nor inappropriate Neither comfortable nor uncomfortable NA From a man aged mid-40s to mid-60s: “Some of the questions are whether you have mold and stuff. That could be a very pointed question for a doctor trying to figure out something about a patient. If they had a problem with mold and they find it in their chart, I think that would be helpful to a doctor and be a go-to. But a question like that, in particular, everything is pertinent to everything else, housing, all these things that this pertains to, and food. It’s all related.” Very appropriate Neither comfortable nor uncomfortable Open in a new tab Abbreviations: EHR, electronic health record; NA, not applicable. Role of Interpersonal Factors in Willingness to Disclose Social Risks Participants discussed the role that interpersonal factors played in their willingness to disclose social risks with health care team members. Several participants said a friendly demeanor would be effective for quickly building rapport and facilitating comfort in disclosing social risks. One participant noted, “Well it depends on the person....Some people are easier to talk to than others. Some people are more pleasant than others.” Two participants also discussed how a long-term patient-physician relationship affected their willingness to discuss social risks. For context, patients at the clinic frequently see a different dermatologist at each visit, and thus a long-term relationship with 1 dermatologist is uncommon. While 1 participant preferred to share social risk information with their primary care physician and other specialists with whom they had a long-term relationship, another participant believed that seeing different dermatologists at each visit decreased their shame about sharing personal socioeconomic needs. A subset of participants who primarily responded that screening was neither appropriate nor inappropriate or somewhat inappropriate described feelings of inadequacy, insecurity, and shame associated with the sensitive nature of the topic and the differences in SES between patients and their physicians. Two participants discussed feeling insecure when discussing social risks in a dermatology clinic, with 1 participant saying there were “wealthy, attractive doctors [who] can go shop at Whole Foods and [have] disposable income” and the other participant, who indicated that screening was very appropriate, commenting, “To admit the fact that you don’t have somewhere to stay is very demeaning personally…it’s something you don’t really want people to know.” Implications of Institutional Trust for Willingness to Disclose and Comfort With Documentation Trust and distrust of institutions, specifically the government and the medical system, affected participants’ views. Trust in the medical system was associated with participants’ willingness to disclose social risks, whereas distrust in the system, which was associated with data surveillance and privacy concerns, was a factor in participants being less comfortable with EHR documentation of social risks. Participants who found screening very or somewhat appropriate discussed that trust in the medical system, including physicians as a whole and the hospital where participants receive care, had a role in their willingness to disclose social risks. These participants viewed all physicians regardless of specialty as “healers” who had their best interests in mind. They also discussed that trust in the hospital played an important role. One participant shared that “[Hospital name] is a place that I’ve been going to my whole life, so I really trust them.…It’s almost like...because of my trust in the institution that I trust the survey.” Participants’ comfort with EHR documentation based on their survey responses diverged from what they shared in their interview. Fear of discrimination and privacy breaches were mentioned by participants with varying degrees of comfort with documentation, including many participants who indicated that they were completely comfortable with documentation. Several participants, including those who were somewhat comfortable, neither comfortable nor uncomfortable, and somewhat uncomfortable, also mentioned the importance of providing future opportunities to update EHRs with their social risk information. They did not want their life circumstances during 1 point in time to be considered permanent. Prior experience with the medical system also led to concerns about documentation of social risks. One participant who was somewhat uncomfortable with EHR documentation shared that a clinician once documented that the participant was “defiant about taking medications” when they expressed hesitancy at adding a new medication. “The physician’s comments [are] actually really, really part of that trust. So you know, I go about thinking things are going well. One day we’re communicating…and then I see this note. It like just throws everything.” Relevance of Screening in a Dermatology Clinic Most participants recognized that screening in a dermatology clinic was relevant as a touchpoint for socioeconomically vulnerable and aging patients or as a way to identify environmental and psychosocial stressors of skin health. However, some participants did not see how social risks were relevant to their dermatologic visit, and another subset of patients noted an expectation of screening in primary care, which they viewed as more concerned with holistic health. Several participants connected the importance of social risks to health and well-being and thus considered screening to be directly relevant to patient care. Two participants pointed out the opportunity in a dermatology clinic to reach patients who tend to “fall through the cracks” (ie, overlooked by the medical system). Other participants mentioned that screening would normalize the discussion of social risks, especially with patients who otherwise may feel ashamed to ask for help or may be unaware that help is available. One participant believed that since dermatologists already discussed sensitive topics, asking about social risks—also sensitive in content—was no different. Many participants acknowledged the association between skin health and nutrition, mold exposure, and stress. For example, 1 participant shared, “Actually, I was comfortable because I felt that they were interested, even asking or having the survey there. And the dermatologist always asks, ‘Are you under stress?’ and that’s one question that will lead to other things that you’re going through.” Multiple participants mentioned that providing an explanation of how their social risks were connected to their dermatologic care would increase their willingness to share this information. Several participants also believed that the clinical team should provide assistance if social risks were disclosed. Examples of such assistance were a personalized resource list and a referral to a social worker. Discussion In this study examining patient perspectives on social risk screening and documentation in a dermatology clinic, 85.9% of participants found screening to be very or somewhat appropriate, which is consistent with previous results of studies in primary care and ED settings. 28 , 29 , 30 , 31 , 32 , 33 , 34 , 35 Sixty-three percent of participants were completely or somewhat comfortable with EHR documentation, which is similar to findings from a multisite study of patients in primary care facilities and EDs. 28 Using a mixed-method design enabled us to discover nuances in participant perspectives that were not captured in the survey data alone. For example, many participants who shared concerns about privacy and discrimination had responded that they were completely comfortable with EHR documentation. We believe that these findings are complementary rather than contradictory. 48 Although patients may have concerns about documenting social risks in the EHR, they may ultimately decide they are comfortable with this practice if it improves their care. Dermatologists can address their patients’ social risks by making adjustments to their care plans. 18 For example, patients who reside in crowded housing or are unhoused may not have a private room in which to apply topical medications or may not have reliable access to washing facilities in which to rinse off topical ointments. Systemic therapy may be a more feasible treatment for these patients. Offering telemedicine visits can help to address transportation challenges and accommodate work and dependent care responsibilities. Dermatologists also have opportunities to assist with connection to resources. Some health care systems subsidize transportation to and from medical appointments. In the study clinic, a patient navigator helps with care coordination, including assistance with obtaining medications, appointment reminders, and connection to health system resources. In the absence of institutional resources, dermatologists can offer information on local community-based resources. In this study, participants’ trust in physicians as a whole and in the hospital played a role in participants’ willingness to discuss social risks, which is similar to other studies. 24 , 25 , 27 , 40 Participants’ distrust in the government and other institutions was associated with privacy and data surveillance concerns and the potential for discrimination based on documented social risks. Dermatologists may find that using a trauma-informed care approach, which emphasizes safety; trustworthiness and transparency; peer support; collaboration and mutuality; empowerment, voice, and choice; and cultural, historical, and gender issues, 49 may help to address patients’ concerns and strengthen their trust in the health care system. In this context, trauma is defined as “an event, series of events, or set of circumstances that is experienced by an individual as physically or emotionally harmful or life threatening and that has lasting adverse effects on the individual’s functioning and mental, physical, social, emotional, or spiritual well-being.” 49 (p7) For example, first, creating a sense of safety within the physical environment could occur through visible messaging of the clinic’s core values and antidiscrimination or antiracism policies. Interpersonal interactions also present opportunities for creating a sense of safety and collaboration within the patient-physician relationship. Second, providing the context for screening in a dermatology clinic and clarifying the purpose of screening may help promote transparency, which is critical to building and maintaining trust. Third, giving patients opportunities to update their EHR data as their social circumstances change would promote empowerment, choice, and voice. These suggestions align with studies that highlight the importance of normalizing the screening process, clarifying the purpose of screening, describing the association between social needs and health, and respecting patient autonomy. 24 , 26 , 40 Limitations This study has several limitations. First, the results are not generalizable to all dermatology settings because the participants were recruited from the dermatology clinic of a single urban public hospital where the dermatology residents rotate every 1 to 2 months. This setting contrasts with most dermatology practices, where there are opportunities to form long-term relationships. Second, due to resource limitations, participants who preferred to speak Spanish and Cantonese were not interviewed and thus their perspectives were not included in data integration. Third, we were unable to examine the qualitative data based on educational level, which was associated with comfort with documentation at a significance of P = .05, because only 1 interviewee had less than 12 years of schooling. Conclusions In this mixed-methods study, most participants found social risk screening to be appropriate in a dermatology clinic, although a smaller proportion of participants were comfortable with EHR documentation of their social risks. Optimizing patients’ trust in their physicians and the medical system, while addressing privacy and discrimination concerns, may help to facilitate disclosure of social risks. Supplement 1. eAppendix. Survey Which Includes the Accountable Health Communities Social Needs Screening Questions eFigure. CONSORT Flow Diagram Click here for additional data file. (349.4KB, pdf) Supplement 2. Data Sharing Statement Click here for additional data file. (13.5KB, pdf) References 1. Penman-Aguilar A, Talih M, Huang D, Moonesinghe R, Bouye K, Beckles G. Measurement of health disparities, health inequities, and social determinants of health to support the advancement of health equity. J Public Health Manag Pract. 2016;22(suppl 1):S33-S42. doi: 10.1097/PHH.0000000000000373 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Braveman P, Egerter S, Williams DR. The social determinants of health: coming of age. Annu Rev Public Health. 2011;32(1):381-398. doi: 10.1146/annurev-publhealth-031210-101218 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. Social determinants of health. September 30, 2021. Accessed April 22, 2022. https://www.cdc.gov/socialdeterminants/index.htm 4. Solar O, Irwin A. A conceptual framework for action on the social determinants of health. World Health Organization. 2010. Accessed April 23, 2022. https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789241500852 5. Alderwick H, Gottlieb LM. Meanings and misunderstandings: a social determinants of 