 Trauma and Justice Strategic Initiative . SAMHSA’s Concept of Trauma and Guidance for a Trauma-Informed Approach. Substance Abuse & Mental Health Services Administration; 2014. Accessed July 29, 2023. https://store.samhsa.gov/sites/default/files/d7/priv/sma14-4884.pdf [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Supplement 1. eAppendix. Survey Which Includes the Accountable Health Communities Social Needs Screening Questions eFigure. CONSORT Flow Diagram Click here for additional data file. (349.4KB, pdf) Supplement 2. Data Sharing Statement Click here for additional data file. (13.5KB, pdf)',125,'2025-10-14 06:29:18','2025-10-14 06:29:18');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2576,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11064734','Analyzing diversity, equity, and inclusion content on dermatology fellowship program websites.','Bohler F, Garden A, Taranikanti V.','2024','diversify micrographic wholistic gmep advertise gmep gmeps gmep gmep micrographic micrographic advertise headers footers devalues gmep gmep oakland costas muniz s40615 traylor schmittdiel uratsu akhiyat cardwell sokumbi clindermatol auseon kolibash capers crites mahler e1900 gox weigel s32 mazmudar vaccarello onamusi ledesma vicioso woreta','ABSTRACT Diversity, Equity, and Inclusion (DEI) initiatives have garnered increasing attention within medical education as there have been increased efforts to diversify the physician workforce among medical students, residents, fellows, and attendings. One way in which programs can improve their DEI initiatives and attract a more diverse pool of applicants is through DEI content on their graduate medical education websites. Prior studies characterizing the content and prevalence of DEI material on residency webpages have shown that dermatology residencies have relatively low levels of DEI content on their websites in which almost ¾ of all programs having no DEI content. Little is known, however, if similar findings are to be expected for the three main dermatology subspecialty fellowship program webpages: Dermatopathology, Pediatric Dermatology, and Micrographic Surgery and Dermatology Oncology. Fellowship programs were identified using the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education’s online database of fellowship programs. Programs were evaluated on a standardized scoring system for five equally weighted criteria: fellowship-specific DEI webpage, DEI commitment statement, DEI initiatives (summer research opportunities for under-represented minorities, DEI council, etc.), link to the institution’s DEI homepage, and information about bias training. The mean score among all programs was 12.5. Pediatric dermatology ranked the highest among all specialties, while Mohs ranked the lowest. A link to the institution’s DEI homepage was the most prevalent factor accounting for 42.1% of all programs collected, whereas information about bias training and fellowship-associated DEI webpage were the least prevalent. The results of this study reveal an overall lack of DEI content across all dermatology subspecialties’ webpages and represent an actionable area of improvement for fellowship directors to increase their DEI efforts to attract a diverse pool of applicants to their program. KEYWORDS: Diversity, equity, and inclusion; DEI; graduate medical education; dermatology; program websites Background Efforts to prioritize diversity, equity, and inclusion (DEI) to address healthcare disparities has become a central focus in medical school, residency, and fellowship programs. Findings have shown racial concordance between physicians and patients can lead to better health outcomes, reducing such disparities among marginalized populations [ 1 , 2 ]. Improving diversity within medicine starts with the recruitment and training of underrepresented groups in both medical school and graduate medical education. Historically, this has been accomplished, in part, through a variety of methods such as race conscious and wholistic admission processes, creating DEI committees, and increasing the visibility of under-represented minorities within their programs [ 3–5 ]. Despite these efforts, the field of dermatology ranks as the second least diverse specialty in medicine [ 3 ]. Since the COVID-19 pandemic, graduate medical education programs (GMEP) have increasingly relied on virtual resources to advertise their program to prospective medical students and residents. GMEP websites are a useful and influential resource that are utilized by prospective applicants to obtain information regarding a program’s training, culture, and overall fit [ 6 , 7 ]. One way in which programs can improve their DEI initiatives in order to potentially attract a more diverse pool of applicants is through DEI content on their GME websites. Findings show that visibility and messaging to prospective applicants are barriers faced by GMEPs when attempting to attract applicants from under-represented groups [ 8 ]. Given that GMEP websites are frequently utilized by prospective applicants to gain a better understanding of a program, inclusion of DEI content on GMEP webpages offer a potential solution to these barriers [ 6 ]. Despite this, prior studies demonstrate that dermatology residency programs’ websites have a dearth of DEI content [ 9 , 10 ]. Little is known, however, if similar findings are to be expected for dermatology fellowship programs. The American Board of Dermatology recognizes three dermatology subspecialties: Dermatopathology, Pediatric Dermatology, and Micrographic Surgery and Dermatologic Oncology (Mohs). The aim of this study is to analyze the presence and characterize the content of DEI information on fellowship websites. Materials and methods Fellowship programs were identified using the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education’s online database of fellowship programs [ 11 ]. Programs that did not have websites were excluded from this study ( n = 19). A total of 152 programs were analyzed. Programs were evaluated on a standardized scoring system for five equally weighted criteria: fellowship-specific DEI webpage, DEI commitment statement, DEI initiatives (summer research opportunities for under-represented minorities, DEI council, etc.), link to the institution’s DEI homepage, and information about bias training. These criteria were derived from previous study designs that investigated similar DEI content in other medical specialties [ 10 , 12 ]. Data was collected independently by two separate individuals for consistency. Any discrepancies between programs’ scores were discussed openly by the authors and, if needed, voted on to reconcile any differences. DEI content on fellowship programs’ websites was quantified utilizing a scoring system ranging from zero to 100 potential points (0 = No DEI content, 20 = one DEI factor present, 40 = two DEI factors present, 60 = three DEI factors present, 80 = four DEI factors present, 100 = All DEI factors present). Within each subspecialty, the mean of these scores for programs was then calculated to determine a subspecialty’s completeness score. Results The mean score among all programs was 12.5 out of a maximum of 100. Pediatric dermatology ranked the highest among all specialties, while Mohs ranked the lowest ( Table 1 ). A link to the institution’s DEI homepage was the most prevalent factor, whereas information about bias training and fellowship-associated DEI webpage were the least prevalent ( Table 1 ). Table 1. Comparison of DEI content between dermatology fellowship program websites. Micrographic Surgery and Dermatologic Oncology (Mohs) ( N =71) Pediatric Dermatology ( N =25) Dermatopathology ( N =56) Total ( N =152) DEI Webpage , n (%) Not Present 67 (94.4%) 24 (96.0%) 56 (100.0%) 147 (96.7%) Present 4 (5.6%) 1 (4.0%) 0 (0.0%) 5 (3.3%) DEI Commitment Statement , n (%) Not Present 65 (91.5%) 23 (92.0%) 52 (92.9%) 140 (92.1%) Present 6 (8.5%) 2 (8.0%) 4 (7.1%) 12 (7.9%) Diversity Initiatives (Summer research for med students, lectures, mentorship) , n (%) Not Present 65 (91.5%) 24 (96.0%) 54 (96.4%) 143 (94.1%) Present 6 (8.5%) 1 (4.0%) 2 (3.6%) 9 (5.9%) Link to the Institution’s DEI Page , n (%) Not Present 54 (76.1%) 10 (40.0%) 24 (42.9%) 88 (57.9%) Present 17 (23.9%) 15 (60.0%) 32 (57.1%) 64 (42.1%) Information about Bias Training , n (%) Not Present 68 (95.8%) 23 (92.0%) 56 (100.0%) 147 (96.7%) Present 3 (4.2%) 2 (8.0%) 0 (0.0%) 5 (3.3%) Completeness Score (out of 100) 10.1 16.8 13.6 12.5 Open in a new tab Discussion While fellowship website DEI content does not fully encompass the totality of a program’s DEI efforts, it can serve as an effective signaling method to prospective residents regarding the emphasis placed on DEI initiatives within a training program. Programs that advertise multiple DEI initiatives are likely to be viewed more favorably by under-represented minority residents and subsequently more likely to train at such institutions. Although 42.1% of fellowship program websites contained a link to the institution’s DEI webpage, most of these links were embedded in the webpages’ headers or footers, which are not unique to a fellowship’s webpage. Since these sections of a webpage are standardized, prospective residents might reasonably assume that this information was not included through the efforts of the fellowship program but rather through institution-wide initiatives. This lack of fellowship-specific DEI initiative to include information on the parts of a program’s webpage that is influenced by fellowship program leadership likely devalues a prospective resident’s perception of the importance of DEI at such program. To further underscore this point, all other DEI factors measured in this study that required direct action by fellowship leadership were included in less than 8% of all program websites. The results of this study reveal an overall lack of DEI content across all dermatology subspecialties’ webpages and represent an actionable area of improvement for fellowship directors to increase their DEI efforts, address barriers to under-represented minority recruitment, and to attract a diverse pool of applicants to their program [ 8 ]. Additionally, future studies should be conducted to assess the full impact of adding DEI content to GMEP websites. This would ideally be accomplished through survey studies of under-represented minorities assessing their perceptions of DEI content on GMEP websites as well as with studies that investigate any changes in minority residents for programs that implement high amounts of DEI content on their webpages. Funding Statement The work was supported by the Oakland University William Beaumont School of Medicine. Disclosure statement No potential conflict of interest was reported by the author(s). References [1]. Shen MJ, Peterson EB, Costas-Muñiz R, et al. The effects of race and racial concordance on patient-physician communication: a systematic review of the literature. J Racial Ethn Health Disparities. 2018;5(1):117–3. doi: 10.1007/s40615-017-0350-4 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [2]. Traylor AH, Schmittdiel JA, Uratsu CS, et al. Adherence to cardiovascular disease medications: does patient-provider race/ethnicity and language concordance matter? J Gen Intern Med. 2010;25(11):1172–1177. doi: 10.1007/s11606-010-1424-8 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [3]. Akhiyat S, Cardwell L, Sokumbi O.. Why dermatology is the second least diverse specialty in medicine: how did we get here? Clin Dermatol. 2020;38(3):310–315. doi: 10.1016/j.clindermatol.2020.02.005 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [4]. Auseon AJ, Kolibash AJ Jr, Capers Q. Successful efforts to increase diversity in a cardiology fellowship training program. Journal Of Graduate Medical Education . 2013;5(3):481–485. doi: 10.4300/JGME-D-12-00307.1 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [5]. Crites K, Johnson J, Scott N, et al. Increasing diversity in residency training programs. Cureus. 2022;14(6). doi: 10.7759/cureus.25962 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [6]. Mahler SA, Wagner MJ, Church A, et al. Importance of residency program web sites to emergency medicine applicants. J Emerg Med. 2009;36(1):83–88. doi: 10.1016/j.jemermed.2007.10.055 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [7]. Chen VW, Hoang D, Garner W. Do websites provide what applicants need? Plastic surgery residency program websites versus applicant self-reported needs. Plast Reconstr Surg Glob Open. 2018;6(10):e1900. doi: 10.1097/GOX.0000000000001900 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [8]. Wilson LT, Weigel B, Ordonez E, et al. Strategies for recruiting underrepresented in medicine and sexual and gender minority students to emergency medicine. AEM Educ Train. 2023;7(S1):S22–S32. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [9]. Mazmudar RS, Vaccarello A, Onamusi T, et al. Availability and content of diversity, equity, and inclusion information on dermatology residency program websites. J Am Acad Dermatol. 2023;88(4):891–893. doi: 10.1016/j.jaad.2022.10.018 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [10]. Wei C, Bernstein SA, Gu A, et al. Evaluating diversity and inclusion content on graduate medical education websites. J Gen Intern Med. 2023;38(3):582–585. doi: 10.1007/s11606-022-07973-9 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] [11]. ACGME - Accreditation Data System (ADS) . [cited 2024 March 24]. Available from: https://apps.acgme.org/ads/Public/Reports/Report/1 [12]. Ledesma Vicioso N, Woreta F, Sun G. Presence of diversity or inclusion information on US ophthalmology residency program websites. JAMA Ophthalmol. 2022;140(6):606–609. doi: 10.1001/jamaophthalmol.2022.1326 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',414,'2025-10-14 06:29:29','2025-10-14 06:29:29');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2577,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2232475','A large-scale evaluation of terminology integration characteristics.','McDonald FS, Chute CG, Ogren PV, Wahner-Roedler D, Elkin PL.','1999','metaphrase colloquial sacrificing chute ansi chute elkin cimino desiderata robboy systematized elkin chute elkin tuttle atkin keck fisk kaihoi monarch lefferts s102 s108 tuttle keck erlbaum sherertz chute elkin atkin kaihoi metaphrase formalization','Abstract OBJECTIVE: To describe terminology integration characteristics of local specialty specific and general vocabularies in order to facilitate the appropriate inclusion and mapping of these terms into a large-scale terminology. METHODS: We compared the sensitivity, specificity, positive predictive value, and positive likelihood ratios for Automated Term Composition to correctly map 9050 local specialty specific (dermatology) terms and 4994 local general terms to UMLS using Metaphrase. Results were systematically combined among exact matches, semantic type filtered matches, and non-filtered matches. For the general set, an analysis of semantic type filtering was performed. RESULTS: Dermatology exact matches defined a sensitivity of 51% (57% for general terms) and a specificity of 86% (92% general terms). Including semantic type filtered matches increased sensitivity (75% dermatology; 88% general); as did inclusion of non-filtered matches (98% and 99%). These inclusions correspondingly decreased specificity (filtered: 82% and 74%; non-filtered: 52% and 32%). Positive predictive values for exact matches (93.0% dermatology, 97.6% general) were improved by small but significant (p < 0.001) margins by including filtered matches (95.1% dermatology, 98.4% general) but decreased with non-filtered matches (89.2% dermatology, 87.8% general). Adding additional semantic types to the filtering algorithm failed to improve the positive predictive value or the positive likelihood ratio of term mapping, in spite of a 2.3% improvement in sensitivity. CONCLUSIONS: Automated methods for mapping local "colloquial" terminologies to large-scale controlled health vocabulary systems are practical (ppv 95% dermatology, 98% general). Semantic type filtering improves specificity without sacrificing sensitivity and yields high positive predictive values in every set analyzed. Full text PDF Previous 864 865 866 867 Next Selected References These references are in PubMed. This may not be the complete list of references from this article. Chute C. G., Cohn S. P., Campbell J. R. A framework for comprehensive health terminology systems in the United States: development guidelines, criteria for selection, and public policy implications. ANSI Healthcare Informatics Standards Board Vocabulary Working Group and the Computer-Based Patient Records Institute Working Group on Codes and Structures. J Am Med Inform Assoc. 1998 Nov-Dec;5(6):503–510. doi: 10.1136/jamia.1998.0050503. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Chute C. G., Elkin P. L. A clinically derived terminology: qualification to reduction. Proc AMIA Annu Fall Symp. 1997:570–574. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Cimino J. J. Desiderata for controlled medical vocabularies in the twenty-first century. Methods Inf Med. 1998 Nov;37(4-5):394–403. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Côté R. A., Robboy S. Progress in medical information management. Systematized nomenclature of medicine (SNOMED). JAMA. 1980 Feb 22;243(8):756–762. doi: 10.1001/jama.1980.03300340032015. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Elkin P. L., Bailey K. R., Chute C. G. A randomized controlled trial of automated term composition. Proc AMIA Symp. 1998:765–769. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Elkin P. L., Mohr D. N., Tuttle M. S., Cole W. G., Atkin G. E., Keck K., Fisk T. B., Kaihoi B. H., Lee K. E., Higgins M. C. Standardized problem list generation, utilizing the Mayo canonical vocabulary embedded within the Unified Medical Language System. Proc AMIA Annu Fall Symp. 1997:500–504. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Evans D. A., Rothwell D. J., Monarch I. A., Lefferts R. G., Cote R. A. Toward representations for medical concepts. Med Decis Making. 1991 Oct-Dec;11(4 Suppl):S102–S108. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Tuttle M. S., Olson N. E., Keck K. D., Cole W. G., Erlbaum M. S., Sherertz D. D., Chute C. G., Elkin P. L., Atkin G. E., Kaihoi B. H. Metaphrase: an aid to the clinical conceptualization and formalization of patient problems in healthcare enterprises. Methods Inf Med. 1998 Nov;37(4-5):373–383. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',68,'2025-10-14 06:29:44','2025-10-14 06:29:44');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2578,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12261851','Enhancing dermatology education: a short-course intervention for final-year medical students.','Osman W, Ibrahim M, Yaqub S, Ibrahim M, Abdalla H, Aljaili G, Abuassa N, Hassan IN.','2025','khartoum underprepared khartoum pragmatically khartoum pigmentary artino fgd fgd transcriptions fgd bahri anonymizing corporis corporis corporis smiley pigmentary rushed echoing sudanese 12909_2025_7649_moesm1_esm fgd sjs bahri lowell froelich federman kirsner dermatologyinprimarycare prevalenceandpatientdisposition jamacaddermatol bolliger dellavalle naldi wulf karimkhani dellavalle coffeng flohr langan nsoesie erskine pmc5817488 ulman pmc4536337 kliesener jandek navarini pmc10848555 dhingra theawaydermatologyelectiveforvisitingmedicalstudents educationalopportunitiesandbarriers dermatolonlinej artino rochelle dezee gehlbach developingquestionnairesforeducationalresearch ameeguideno medteach epub2014mar24 pmc4059192 ardent whatisthekirkpatrickmodel learnthe4levelsofevaluation retrieved15march2025from ardentlearning bala e288 ski2 pmc10690650 succar mccluskey grigg enhancingmedicalstudenteducationbyimplementingacompetency basedophthalmologycurriculum asiapacjophthalmol 2017jan cherasco papotti abatedaga abatedaga massimiliano veglio frafranco impactofsimulation basedandflippedclassroomlearningonself perceivedclinicalskillscomparedtotraditionaltraining educationsciences educsci15010031 goodboy compedu andewi hastomo aquasi experimentalresearch journalofenglisheducation v11i3 scorer doody badran keraa farghaly 12909_2025_7649_moesm1_esm','Abstract Background Dermatological conditions are prevalent in outpatient consultations worldwide, representing up to 24% of primary care visits. However, dermatology education is often underrepresented in medical curricula, particularly in low- and middle-income countries (LMICs) like Sudan. This study aimed to evaluate the impact of a short dermatology course on final-year medical students’ knowledge and confidence, with the aim of informing curriculum development in Sudan. Methods A mixed-methods interventional study was conducted with 50 final-year medical students at a public university in Khartoum, Sudan. Participants underwent a 15-hour dermatology course, covering essential knowledge and diagnostic skills tailored to the local disease burden. Pre- and post-course assessments were administered using a self-administered structured questionnaire. Quantitative data were analyzed using SPSS, and qualitative data from a focus group discussion were analyzed using an inductive open-coding approach. Results The study found significant improvements in participants’ knowledge and diagnostic skills. Knowledge increased from an average of 48.2–83.4% ( p < 0.001), and diagnostic skills improved from 54.6 to 90.2% ( p < 0.001). The course notably enhanced students’ ability to identify fungal infections, malignant lesions, and dermatologic emergencies. Focus group discussions revealed high satisfaction with the course’s content, structure, and delivery, with students expressing increased confidence in managing dermatologic conditions during clinical practice. Conclusion A short dermatology course significantly improved knowledge and diagnostic confidence among final-year medical students in Sudan. Given the limited dermatology exposure in undergraduate curricula, such targeted educational interventions can bridge knowledge gaps and better prepare students for clinical practice in resource-limited settings. Future studies should explore long-term retention and practical skill assessment to further strengthen dermatology education. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1186/s12909-025-07649-y. Keywords: Dermatology education, Medical students, Knowledge improvement, Diagnostic skills intervention strategies Background Dermatological conditions are among the most common reasons for outpatient consultations worldwide, accounting for up to 24% of primary care visits in some settings [ 1 ]. Despite this, dermatology is often underrepresented in undergraduate medical curricula, particularly in low- and middle-income countries (LMICs), where the burden of skin diseases is disproportionately high [ 2 , 3 ]. In many medical schools, dermatology training is brief and largely theoretical, leaving graduates underprepared to diagnose and manage common skin conditions [ 4 ]. This educational gap is especially concerning in countries like Sudan, where access to specialist dermatology services is limited and primary care physicians play a key role in diagnosing and managing skin conditions. Studies have shown that short, targeted educational interventions can significantly improve medical students’ knowledge, confidence, and clinical skills in dermatology [ 5 , 6 ]. Such courses offer a practical solution to curriculum constraints, helping bridge the gap between academic knowledge and real-world clinical competency. In Sudan, final-year medical students typically receive minimal structured dermatology education—usually limited to a few lectures without hands-on clinical exposure. This lack of training has the potential to affect both diagnostic accuracy and patient care, especially in rural and underserved areas where general physicians are often the first point of contact. The aim of this study was to evaluate the impact of a short dermatology course on the knowledge and confidence of final-year medical students at a public university in Sudan. The findings aim to inform future curriculum development and advocate for stronger integration of dermatology into undergraduate medical education. Methods Study design and participants A mixed-methods interventional study was conducted among 50 final-year medical students at a public university in Khartoum, Sudan. Participants were recruited by inviting volunteers from the cohort of final-year medical students, following a short presentation outlining the purpose and design of the study. The target number of 50 participants was based on the available final-year cohort and reflected the maximum feasible sample for this single-institution pilot study. This sample size was determined pragmatically rather than through formal power calculation. All participants provided written informed consent prior to enrollment. Development and outline of the course A 15-hour short course in dermatology was developed by expert consultant dermatologists. The initial step involved identifying the essential knowledge and diagnostic skills medical graduates need in dermatology. The course content was based on the core curriculum recommendations of the British Association of Dermatologists (BAD) and adapted to the local disease burden. Topics, course content, and instructional activities were then developed accordingly. The final course design was reviewed by a medical education specialist. The course was delivered in five sessions, each lasting 3 h, over a two-week period (two sessions per week, with the final session dedicated to review and feedback). The course took place in a well-equipped teaching venue in Khartoum, Sudan. Sessions were delivered by two senior consultant dermatologists and moderated by a medical educator and a clinical tutor. The expected learning outcomes were that participants would be able to: (1) Identify and classify common skin diseases (infectious, inflammatory, pigmentary, and neoplastic), (2) Recognize dermatologic emergencies and referral criteria, (3) Describe first-line management of prevalent skin disorders, (4) Understand basic pediatric and drug-related dermatoses, (5) Demonstrate improved diagnostic confidence through case-based image interpretation. The teaching strategy included lectures, image-based case discussions, and small group work, delivered within a flipped classroom approach. Students were required to study pre-class materials (readings and dermatology video modules) prior to attending sessions. This self-directed component aimed to enhance student motivation, autonomy, and clinical confidence. In-class sessions emphasized active participation and interactive problem-solving. Data collection and analysis Data were collected at two time points: at the beginning of the course and one month after its completion, using a self-administered structured questionnaire composed of 20 closed-ended items. The same questionnaire was used for both pre- and post-course assessments. It was developed using guidance from AMEE Guide No. 87: Developing questionnaires for educational research by Artino et al. [ 7 ], and reviewed by two dermatology experts to ensure content validity. The questionnaire assessed: (1) Knowledge of common dermatologic conditions and management, (2) Visual diagnostic skills using image-based scenarios, (3) Self-reported confidence in diagnosing and managing dermatologic cases. An English-language version of the complete questionnaire is available as Supplementary File 1 . A pilot test was conducted among five medical students not involved in the main study to ensure clarity of the questionnaire items. Due to resource constraints, internal consistency metrics such as Cronbach’s alpha were not calculated. This limitation has been acknowledged in the discussion. A focus group discussion (FGD) was conducted at the end of the course with a purposive sample of 8–10 students. The FGD guide was developed based on levels 1 (reaction) and 2 (learning) of Kirkpatrick’s four-level training model [ 8 ]. The discussion was facilitated by a trained moderator independent of the study team and lasted approximately 60 min. It explored students’ perceptions of course content, teaching strategies, learning experience, and suggestions for improvement. The session was audio-recorded, transcribed verbatim, and checked for accuracy by the facilitator. The transcriptions were anonymized before being analyzed. Quantitative data were entered into Microsoft Excel and analyzed using SPSS version 26.0. The Fisher exact test was used to examine associations between participation in the course and improvements in knowledge and skills. A p-value of less than 0.05 was considered statistically significant. Qualitative data from the FGD were analyzed using an inductive open-coding approach to identify key themes. Ethical considerations Ethical approval for this study was obtained from University of Bahri Faculty of Medicine Institutional Review Board in Sudan. Participation was voluntary, and all students provided written informed consent prior to enrollment in the study. Confidentiality was strictly maintained by anonymizing all data, including pre- and post-assessment scores. Physical documents were stored securely in a locked cabinet, and digital data were protected with password-secured files. The collected data were used solely for educational and research purposes, and no identifying information was disclosed in the dissemination of the findings. Results Quantitative data Among the 50 final-year medical students who participated in this study, 30 were females and 20 were males. The mean age of the study participants was 23.7 years. All participants attended the full course and completed both the pre- and post-course assessments. As shown in (Fig. 1 ), the pre-course and post-course assessments revealed marked gains in all areas. The average knowledge improved from 48.2% on the pre-course assessment to 83.4% on the post-course assessment. The most noticeable improvement was in the knowledge related to fungal skin infections, which improved by 44%, followed by the knowledge of malignant skin lesion recognition, which improved by 39%. The Fisher exact test revealed a significant association between attending the course and improvement in overall knowledge ( p < 0.001) (Table 1 ). Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Overall Improvement in Knowledge, Diagnostic Skills, and Intervention Confidence After the Dermatology Short Course This figure illustrates the significant improvements in participants’ knowledge, diagnostic skills, and confidence in managing dermatological conditions following the completion of the short course. The pre-course and post-course assessments show marked gains across all areas, highlighting the effectiveness of the intervention Table 1. Pre- and post-course difference in participants’ knowledge about dermatology Intervention for Condition Pre-course % Correct Post-course % Correct Difference (+) Fungal skin infections 38% 82% 44% Eczema and dermatitis 52% 88% 36% Acne and common pediatric dermatoses 60% 92% 32% Recognition of malignant lesions 33% 72% 39% Management principles 58% 86% 28% Overall Average 48.2% 83.4% 35.2% Open in a new tab p < 0.001 by Fisher exact test This table presents the comparison of participants’ knowledge scores before and after the dermatology short course. Significant improvements were observed in all areas assessed, with the highest gains noted in the recognition of fungal infections and malignant skin lesions. Statistical significance was determined using the Fisher exact test ( p < 0.001) The average diagnostic skills among the participants improved from 54.6% on the pre-course to 90.2% on the post-course assessment. The largest improvement after the course was in the diagnostic ability related to dermatologic emergencies such as Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (50% improvement), followed by the skills of identifying tinea corporis (42% improvement). The Fisher exact test revealed a significant association between attending the course and improvement in overall diagnostic skills ( p < 0.001) (Table 2 ). Table 2. Pre- and post-course difference in diagnostic skills Intervention for Condition Pre-course % Correct Post-course % Correct Difference (+) Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (emergency) 30% 80% 50% Tinea corporis 40% 82% 42% Psoriasis 65% 92% 27% Atopic dermatitis 70% 94% 24% Overall Average 54.6% 90.2% 35.6% Open in a new tab p < 0.001 by Fisher exact test This table outlines the changes in participants’ diagnostic skills before and after the dermatology short course. The results show a substantial improvement in participants’ ability to diagnose dermatologic emergencies and common skin conditions, with the most significant gains in recognizing conditions like Stevens-Johnson Syndrome and tinea corporis. Statistical significance was assessed using the Fisher exact test ( p < 0.001) Participants’ knowledge regarding intervention strategies for common dermatological conditions improved from 56.2% on the pre-course assessment to 91.6% after the course. The chief improvement was in the appropriate management of eczema and fungal infections (38% and 36% improvements, respectively). The Fisher exact test revealed a significant association between attending the course and improvement in overall knowledge of interventional actions ( p < 0.001) (Table 3 ). Table 3. Pre- and post-course difference in participants’ knowledge of interventions needed to treat common skin conditions Intervention for Condition Pre-course % Correct Post-course % Correct Difference (+) Management of eczema 50% 88% 38% Treatment of fungal infections 52% 88% 36% First-line management of acne 65% 90% 25% Referral criteria for skin cancer 58% 90% 32% Overall Average 56.2% 91.6% 35.4% Open in a new tab p < 0.001 by Fisher exact test This table compares participants’ knowledge of appropriate intervention strategies for treating common skin conditions before and after the dermatology short course. Post-course assessments demonstrated significant improvement in the understanding of management strategies, particularly for eczema and fungal infections. The Fisher exact test was used to assess statistical significance ( p < 0.001) Qualitative data A focus group discussion was conducted with 10 randomly selected participants immediately following the final session. The qualitative data are presented below using the framework of Kirkpatrick’s first two levels of training evaluation: Level 1 (Reaction) and Level 2 (Learning) (Fig. 2 ) [ 8 ]. Fig. 2. Open in a new tab Kirkpatrick’s four-level training model The model illustrates the progression from training to organizational impact through four levels: (1) Reaction: Participants’ initial engagement (smiley/feedback icon). (2) Learning: Knowledge gained (book/brain icon). (3) Behavior: Application of knowledge (stethoscope/action icon). (4) Results: Organizational outcomes (bar chart icon) The focus group interview guide consisted of five questions with several planned probes: Level 1 (reactions): What is your general impression of this course? What do you think the organizers of the course could do better? (Probe for examples such as duration, content, or practical arrangements) Is there anything you would like to share about how this course was taught? (Probe for preferences regarding teaching style, group work, etc.) Level 2 (learning): 4. What did you find useful in this course? (Probe for details about specific content or teaching methods) 5. What would you like to share about the content of this course? (Probe for specific sessions or presenters) Reaction-level findings Participants expressed high satisfaction with the course content, structure, and delivery. The course was described as “engaging,” “practical,” and “very different from regular dermatology lectures.” Students appreciated the flipped classroom approach, noting that pre-session videos and readings helped them participate more actively in class. One student commented: This was the first time I actually felt confident in dermatology… seeing real cases and discussing them with consultants helped me connect theory to practice. Participants viewed the teaching environment as comfortable and conducive to learning. While most sessions were well-received, students suggested that more time should be allocated to dermatologic emergencies and pigmentary disorders. One participant noted: The session on drug eruptions was a bit rushed—there were many types, and it would help to discuss them over more time. The course was also described as “intensive,” and some students suggested that the content could be spread out over a longer period—such as one session per week—to allow more time for reflection and consolidation. Additionally, many students recommended the inclusion of live patient demonstrations or simulated clinical stations in future courses to further enhance realism and clinical applicability. Learning-level findings Participants reported significant improvements in their understanding of dermatologic conditions and their diagnostic capability. Many shared that the course helped them distinguish between benign, complicated, and urgent skin conditions—an area of uncertainty prior to the intervention. Several students also noted improved ability to choose appropriate treatments for common conditions like eczema and fungal infections. Students felt more confident and better equipped to manage dermatologic complaints during internship or in general practice. One remarked: Before this, I would refer most skin cases. Now I know which I can handle and which need a specialist. While participants acknowledged the value of learning key dermatologic concepts, they also felt that less time could be spent on theoretical details, such as complex pathophysiology, and more emphasis placed on clinical application. They unanimously agreed that dermatology should receive more structured and practical teaching time in the undergraduate curriculum and recommended making this short course a standard component for future medical student cohorts. Discussion This study was conducted to determine whether a short course in dermatology could significantly enhance medical students’ knowledge and diagnostic confidence regarding common skin conditions. The results clearly demonstrate that such an intervention has a positive impact on students’ knowledge, diagnostic skills, and perceived readiness to manage dermatological presentations in clinical practice. The participants’ overall knowledge improved significantly after the course. The most notable gains were in the recognition of fungal skin infections and malignant lesions, which are fundamental in both primary and emergency care settings. These findings are consistent with prior studies emphasizing that focused teaching can substantially improve dermatologic diagnostic accuracy among non-specialists [ 9 , 10 ]. The American Academy of Dermatology (AAD) underscores the importance of equipping medical students with core dermatology competencies, including the ability to recognize life-threatening skin conditions and manage common dermatoses [ 11 ]. There was also a noticeable and statistically significant improvement in diagnostic skills. The greatest improvement occurred in the identification of dermatologic emergencies such as Stevens-Johnson Syndrome. These conditions are time-sensitive and often misdiagnosed in general practice settings, leading to delayed referrals and worse outcomes [ 5 ]. This underscores the importance of training future physicians to recognize urgent cutaneous presentations early, especially in resource-limited environments. Participation in the course significantly enhanced students’ knowledge regarding appropriate interventions for common skin conditions. This was particularly evident in the management of eczema and superficial fungal infections, two of the most prevalent dermatologic complaints encountered in primary care [ 9 , 11 ]. The inclusion of such topics ensures that students gain the ability to manage mild to moderate conditions independently, while recognizing when to escalate care. The flipped classroom approach used in this course proved to be highly effective. Students reported greater engagement, retention, and satisfaction with the learning experience, which aligns with studies supporting the flipped model’s role in promoting deeper understanding and application of knowledge [ 12 – 14 ]. Moreover, active learning strategies like case-based discussions and visual diagnosis exercises improved student confidence and motivation [ 8 , 12 ]. On the contrary, students were less satisfied with sessions that relied heavily on lecture-style delivery and dense slide presentations—echoing concerns raised in earlier research showing minimal learning benefit from passive PowerPoint-heavy formats [ 13 ]. Students appreciated the comprehensive content and felt more confident managing skin-related cases during internships. This finding parallels earlier studies demonstrating that dermatology-focused interventions increase academic performance and self-assurance in real clinical encounters [ 10 , 15 ]. However, participants recommended reducing the time spent on detailed theoretical content—especially complex pathophysiology—and increasing the emphasis on practical learning through visual diagnosis exercises, live patient demonstrations, or simulated cases. These suggestions are consistent with calls to reform dermatology education toward more competency-based frameworks [ 9 , 15 ]. Although it is difficult to define an optimal duration for undergraduate dermatology training, the improvements in both knowledge and skills reported in this 15-hour intervention suggest that even short, targeted courses can be impactful. Several students also expressed that the intensive format may be too demanding for some institutions or learners, and a longer, weekly schedule might facilitate deeper reflection and improved knowledge retention. This is an area worth exploring in subsequent iterations of the course. Nonetheless, the ideal structure and duration of such modules warrant further study. The incorporation of simulated patients or bedside teaching, as suggested by participants, could allow evaluation of behavioral outcomes (Kirkpatrick’s Level 3), adding a valuable dimension to future interventions [ 8 , 16 ]. In addition, our evaluation did not assess Kirkpatrick’s Level 3 (behavioral change) or Level 4 (organizational impact), which are essential for understanding whether short-course interventions lead to sustained clinical behavior or institutional improvements. These should be key considerations in future studies. To our knowledge, this is one of the first structured dermatology education interventions targeting final-year medical students in Sudan. The results offer valuable insights into how low-cost, scalable teaching models can address curriculum gaps in dermatology, particularly in LMICs where such training is often underrepresented. In low-resource settings such as Sudan, where access to dermatology specialists is concentrated in urban centers, empowering general practitioners with strong dermatology foundations is essential. With the majority of the population living in underserved areas, effective primary-level care for skin diseases can help prevent complications, reduce referrals, and improve health outcomes [ 5 , 9 ]. Limitations This study has a few limitations worth noting. First, the study was conducted at a single institution with a relatively small group of 50 final-year medical students, which may limit generalizability to other medical schools, especially those with different curricular frameworks or resource levels. Additionally, we did not collect detailed demographic data such as prior dermatology exposure, which limits our ability to assess baseline variability and how prior experience may have influenced outcomes. Nonetheless, the participants reflect the typical educational background and curriculum exposure of Sudanese public medical schools. Second, while the pre- and post-intervention assessments effectively captured changes in knowledge, they did not evaluate practical dermatological skills or clinical performance. Incorporating hands-on assessments or clinical simulations would have provided a more comprehensive evaluation of learning outcomes. Finally, the one-month follow-up period was relatively short and did not allow for the evaluation of long-term knowledge retention, clinical performance, or real-world behavioral application. Furthermore, no practical skill assessments such as Objective Structured Clinical Examinations (OSCEs) or direct observation of clinical encounters were conducted, limiting our ability to evaluate hands-on competency. As participation was voluntary, there is a potential for self-selection bias, with more motivated or interested students likely to enroll. Moreover, the absence of a control group restricts causal inference, as observed improvements may partly reflect test-retest effects or heightened participant awareness (Hawthorne effect). Despite these limitations, the study provides preliminary evidence supporting the value of structured dermatology short courses in undergraduate medical education. Conclusion This study demonstrates that a structured short-course intervention can significantly enhance dermatology knowledge among final-year medical students. Given the limited dermatology exposure in undergraduate curricula, such targeted educational programs can serve as an effective strategy to bridge knowledge gaps and better prepare students for clinical practice. Future efforts should focus on expanding this approach to other institutions and incorporating practical skill assessments to further strengthen dermatology education in resource-limited settings. Electronic supplementary material Below is the link to the electronic supplementary material. 12909_2025_7649_MOESM1_ESM.docx (10.9KB, docx) Supplementary Material 1: English version of the dermatology short-course assessment questionnaire used in the study. The questionnaire includes 15 multiple-choice knowledge questions, 5 image-based diagnostic scenarios (described), and 10 self-assessment items measuring confidence in clinical dermatology tasks. Abbreviations MCQ Multiple Choice Question SD Standard Deviation SPSS Statistical Package for the Social Sciences IQR Interquartile Range OSCE Objective Structured Clinical Examination KAP Knowledge, Attitude, and Practice P-value Probability Value FGD Focus group discussion LMICs Low- and middle-income countries AAD The American Academy of Dermatology BAD British Association of Dermatologists SJS Stevens-Johnson Syndrome Author contributions WO: Writing– review & editing, Supervision, Project administration, Conceptualization. MI: Writing– original draft, Writing– review & editing, Project administration, Methodology, Data curation, Conceptualization. SY: Writing– review & editing, Methodology, Formal analysis, Conceptualization. MI, HA, GA: Project administration, Investigation, Data curation. NA: Writing– review & editing. INH: Writing– original draft, Writing– review & editing, Project administration, Methodology, Investigation, Formal analysis, Data curation, Conceptualization. All authors read and approved the final manuscript. Funding The author(s) received no financial support for the research, authorship, and/or publication of this article. Data availability The datasets used and/or analyzed during the current study are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request. Declarations Ethics approval and consent to participate Ethical approval was obtained from University of Bahri Faculty of Medicine Institutional Review Board in Sudan. Each participant was asked to sign informed written consent prior to study. This study was conducted in accordance with the ethical principles of the Declaration of Helsinki. Consent for publication Not applicable. Competing interests The authors declare no competing interests. Clinical trial number Not applicable. Footnotes Publisher’s note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. Lowell BA, Froelich CW, Federman DG, Kirsner RS. Dermatologyinprimarycare:Prevalenceandpatientdisposition.JAmAcadDermatol.2001;45(2):250-5.10.1067/mjd.2001.114598.PMID:11464187. 2. Hay RJ, Johns NE, Williams HC, Bolliger IW, Dellavalle RP, Margolis DJ, Marks R, Naldi L, Weinstock MA, Wulf SK, Michaud C, Murray JL. The global burden of skin disease in 2010: an analysis of the prevalence and impact of skin conditions. J Invest Dermatol. 2014;134(6):1527–34. 10.1038/jid.2013.446. Epub 2013 Oct 28. PMID: 24166134. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Karimkhani C, Dellavalle RP, Coffeng LE, Flohr C, Hay RJ, Langan SM, Nsoesie EO, Ferrari AJ, Erskine HE, Silverberg JI, Vos T, Naghavi M. Global skin disease morbidity and mortality: an update from the global burden of disease study 2013. JAMA Dermatol. 2017;153(5):406–12. 10.1001/jamadermatol.2016.5538. PMID: 28249066; PMCID: PMC5817488. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Ulman CA, Binder SB, Borges NJ. Assessment of medical students’ proficiency in dermatology: are medical students adequately prepared to diagnose and treat common dermatologic conditions in the united states?? J Educ Eval Health Prof. 2015;12:18. PMID: 25989840; PMCID: PMC4536337. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Kliesener T, Jandek M, Navarini A, Brandt O, Müller S. Dermatology teaching for undergraduate medical students in clinical routine - a structured four-week curriculum. BMC Med Educ. 2024;24(1):116. 10.1186/s12909-023-04921-x. PMID: 38321407; PMCID: PMC10848555. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Philips RC, Dhingra N, Uchida T, Wagner RF Jr. Theawaydermatologyelectiveforvisitingmedicalstudents:Educationalopportunitiesandbarriers.DermatolOnlineJ.2009;15(10):1.PMID:19951619. 7. Artino AR Jr, La Rochelle JS, Dezee KJ, Gehlbach H. Developingquestionnairesforeducationalresearch:AMEEGuideNo.87.MedTeach.2014;36(6):463– 74.doi:10.3109/0142159X.2014.889814.Epub2014Mar24.PMID:24661014;PMCID:PMC4059192. 8. Ardent Learning.WhatistheKirkpatrickmodel?Learnthe4levelsofevaluation. 2020.Retrieved