INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2592,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7720814','Using Implementation Science to Optimize the Uptake of Evidence-Based Medicine into Dermatology Practice.','Ashrafzadeh S, Metlay JP, Choudhry NK, Emmons KM, Asgari MM.','2019','diverges tackled chren arron bonafide keren harboe asgari blechman dellavalle handley outdoors tanning playground shade punishments sunburn phase5 crux damschroder sacrificing pragmatism kreuter klesges kreuter tabak presumption feldstein incentivizing abound terbinafine mikailov terbinafine randomizing soltanipoor dispensers moisturizing moisturizing creams enrollee compromized lindelof marcil kitson skincare kottner 2019a 2019b showcasing soliciting karas k24 ar069760 coldiron storrs acms asdsa asms micrographic choosingwisely list1 asgari micrographic harboe damschroder hagedorn kilbourne blechman micrographic bonafide keren deimplementation cfirguide stange sustainment chren arron damschroder aron kirsh lowery virtuous ceasing desisting feldstein egbert swetter reexamining reexcision transected fic researchtopics implementationscience vinson boles kreuter 1_suppl handley gorukanti cattamanchi ory estabrooks schreckenberger swetter curiel lewandrowski grichnik kitson rycroft seers titchen parihs klesges estabrooks dzewaltowski kottner hahnel genedy balzer skincare lindelof sigurgeirsson gabel marcil mikailov mostaghimi wooding cancercontrol 2019a cancercontrol tidirc 2019b obssr obssr tidirh alonge agyepong f6753 silmere raghavan hovmand aarons bunger encabo belinchon soltanipoor rustemeyer sluiter frison kezic tabak khoong uspreventiveservicestaskforce recommendationstatementfinal screening2 uspreventiveservicestaskforce updatesummaryfinal counseling2 gillings wustl democratizing dellavalle','Abstract An estimated 17-year lag exists between evidence generation and its integration into routine clinical care. The field of implementation science has emerged to close this gap by applying rigorous methods to systematically study the obstacles and facilitators of the uptake of evidence-based practices. However, implementation science has not gained wide traction in dermatology. In this narrative review, we use literature and expert input to introduce implementation science and key frameworks for implementing interventions and evaluating their uptake. We then highlight opportunities for dermatology-specific interventions at the patient-, provider-, system-, and population-levels, and advocate for the field’s expansion into dermatology. DEFINING IMPLEMENTATION SCIENCE There is an estimated 17-year lag in the incorporation of evidence into routine clinical care ( Morris et al., 2011 ), and as a consequence, care that is delivered in everyday practice often diverges from that which is evidence-based. Numerous evidence-based guidelines are available within dermatology, including those put forth by the American Academy of Dermatology, the British Association of Dermatologists, and Cochrane Skin Group. Yet, the degree to which dermatologists adhere to guidelines and best practices is largely unknown because few studies have assessed the concordance between clinical evidence and actual practice within the field. Obstacles to the implementation of well-established evidence in dermatology must be anticipated and tackled now to ensure optimal care delivery and population health outcomes ( Chren and Arron, 2016 ). A comprehensive solution to overcoming research-to-practice gaps rests on the successful identification of those gaps and a systemic approach to addressing them. Implementation science, as defined by the National Institutes of Health, is the systematic study of processes and factors designed to promote the “adoption and integration of evidence-based practices, interventions, and policies into routine health care and public health settings” ( Fogarty International Center, 2019 ). The field’s overarching aim is to facilitate the deployment of evidence-based interventions in a target population ( Bauer et al., 2015 ). Just as implementation science seeks to enhance the incorporation of well-documented evidence into clinical practice, de-implementation is a related field that functions conceptually as the other side of the same coin: to eliminate low-value treatments and services that lack effectiveness ( Bonafide and Keren, 2018 ; Davidson et al. 2017 ). Similar to implementation science, de-implementation can effectively avoid or mitigate harm to patients, improve processes of care, and reduce healthcare costs. Choosing Wisely is a US-based health educational campaign led by the American Board of Internal Medicine Foundation that has partnered with medical specialty societies, including the American Academy of Dermatology, to formulate and disseminate 10 recommendations for avoiding unnecessary tests, treatments and procedures ( American Academy of Dermatology Workgroup, 2019 ) – a de-implementation effort. Many opportunities exist to carry out de-implementation in dermatology; for example, there is a need to address the overuse of perioperative antibiotics for dermatologic surgeries ( Bae-Harboe and Liang, 2013 ) and a need to reduce inappropriate usage of Mohs surgery ( Ad Hoc Task Force et al., 2012 ; Asgari et al., 2012 ; Blechman et al., 2014 ). Barriers to conducting implementation or de-implementation studies in dermatology include a lack of understanding of the utility and methods for conducting this type of research, the misconception that simply generating evidence is enough to change clinical practice, and the need for a more robust evidence-base within the field grounded in randomized controlled trials and comparative effectiveness studies that can better define the gap between evidence and current care ( Romero-Pérez et al., 2016 ; Williams, 2014 , 2013 ; Williams and Dellavalle, 2012 ). Based on published literature and the coauthors’ experiences in the research field, our goal in this article is to provide a broad overview of implementation science for dermatologists, review key methods and frameworks, and highlight resources and funding opportunities with the goal of paving the path for future implementation studies in dermatology. THE IMPLEMENTATION RESEARCH PROCESS The scope of implementation science is broad and far-reaching in comparison to traditional clinical research; it targets not just the patient but providers, healthcare systems, and policymakers. As such, conducting implementation research requires a multidisciplinary team with members from diverse fields, such as health services research, epidemiology, health economics, psychology, organizational behavior, and operations management. The research process can be conceptualized in a stepwise approach ( Figure 1 ). Pre-implementation planning begins with identifying the evidence-based practice that the researcher seeks to implement, then defining performance gaps (how the current practice differs from the ideal practice) and outcome gaps (how real-world outcomes differ from outcomes expected under evidence-based care) ( Handley et al., 2016 ). Relevant stakeholders need to be appropriately identified and their willingness to change needs to be assessed. Pre-implementation planning sets the stage for the design of the implementation strategy, which usually involves the application of a specific theory and/or framework that addresses both internal and external contextual factors. Implementation outcomes should be clearly defined in advance, and should include variables such as acceptability to stakeholders; appropriateness, or degree of fit of an intervention to a particular context; and coverage, or the degree to which a target population receives an intervention ( Peters et al., 2013 ; Proctor et al., 2011 ). The implementation intervention should be launched in the appropriate context (typically a real-world setting) and should be targeted to the appropriate level (patient, provider, system, or population). Figure 1. Open in a new tab The Implementation Research Process. The implementation science research process can be conceptualized in four general steps. In the first step, pre-implementation planning, investigators identify an evidence-based practice, then define performance gaps and outcome gaps. Relevant stakeholders are identified and their willingness to change is assessed. Based on these assessments, investigators design their intervention strategy by choosing an appropriate framework and relevant implementation outcomes, including outcomes assessing intervention acceptability to stakeholders. Investigators then launch the intervention at the appropriate population level (patient, provider, system, or population) and assess implementation outcomes. IMPLEMENTATION STUDIES DIFFER FROM HEALTH SERVICES RESEARCH While health services research examines the effectiveness of therapeutics, implementation studies examine the facilitators and barriers to implementing findings from health services research in clinical contexts that may differ from the context in which the health services research was conducted. In other words, implementation science centers around the rate and quality of the use of an evidence-based practice in a broad range of health systems ( Bauer et al., 2015 ). For example, in dermatology, organ-transplant recipients face a substantially higher risk of non-melanoma skin cancer owing to the effects of systemic immunosuppression. An effectiveness study might measure rates of skin cancer among organ-transplant recipients who use various forms of UV protection in order to establish evidence for the relative benefits of each. An implementation study, on the other hand, could seek to integrate skin cancer prevention care delivery into transplant units once clear evidence of the benefits has been ascertained and subsequently measure the rate of adoption and maintenance of preventive behavior. It is essential to distinguish between studying implementation rather than the effectiveness of an intervention when designing an implementation study. FRAMEWORKS FOR IMPLEMENTATION STUDIES Clear and consistent use of frameworks can help inform implementation science studies by providing an appropriate and effective means to implement the intervention and evaluate outcomes. Multiple different frameworks are available to guide each phase of the research process. The criteria for framework selection include the level of intervention, inclusion of change strategies, and outcomes of interest. We describe commonly used frameworks in implementation science below ( Table 1 ). Table 1. Common Frameworks for Assessing, Implementing, and Evaluating Interventions Framework Considerations for Use CFIR Intervention Characteristics Is the quality and validity of the evidence supporting the intervention sound? Is the intervention adaptable? Specifies determinants that act as enablers or barriers of implementation outcomes Helps understand what factors influence implementation outcomes and/or explain the variation in outcomes Useful for successful implementation of interventions Outer Setting Does the organization implementing the intervention know and prioritize addressing the needs of the target population? Is the organization well-networked with external organizations? Inner Setting Does the organization have an implementation climate? Are leaders and managers in the organization committed towards supporting the intervention? Individual Characteristics What are the target population’s attitudes and values toward the intervention? Do individuals believe they are capable of completing actions targeted by the intervention? Process of Implementation Is the implementation method for the intervention well thought out? Are there leaders who are responsible for implementing the interventions, and are there "champions" dedicated to support the intervention? PRECEDE Phase 1: Social Assessment What are the social factors (e.g. outdoors job) that affect quality of life in the target population? Defines aspects of implementation to evaluate in order to determine implementation success In conjunction with PROCEED (below), useful for providing analysis on each step of the implementation Often used to evaluate health-promotion and disease prevention programs, especially at the community- and population-levels Phase 2: Epidemiological, Behavioral & Environmental Assessment What specific health needs exist in the target population? What individual and peer behaviors (e.g. tanning) affect the social and health problems of the individuals at risk? What environmental factors (e.g. strong sunlight in outdoor playground with no shade shelter) are linked to behaviors that affect the individuals at risk? Phase 3: Educational & Ecological Assessment What are the predisposing factors (i.e. individual’s knowledge, values), reinforcing factors (i.e. rewards or punishments due to behavior, e.g. blistering sunburn from prolonged sun exposure), and enabling factors (i.e. facilitate behavior uptake) that contribute to the behavior and environment? Phase 4: Administrative & Policy Assessment What health services, health promotion initiatives, and policies need to be addressed before the intervention is implemented? PROCEED Phase5: Implementation Implementation of the intervention. The PRECEDE and PROCEED models are often used together. See “PRECEDE” above for when to use the PROCEED model. Phase 6: Process Evaluation Is the intervention being implemented according to the protocol or do modifications need to be made to improve the intervention? Phase 7: Impact Evaluation How effective is the intervention on influencing predisposing, enabling, and reinforcing factors, and on influencing behavior? Phase 8: Outcome Evaluation How effective is the intervention in altering the target population’s quality of life? RE-AIM Reach To what extent does the intervention penetrate the target population? Useful for evaluating implementation success, and may be easier to use since has fewer components Often used in health promotion to determine if an intervention has the desired effect Effectiveness What is the impact, including unintended effects, of the intervention on outcomes? Adoption Who is willing and able to initiate/adopt the program? Implementation How consistently are the elements of the intervention protocol being followed? Maintenance To what extent has the intervention persisted, become institutionalized, or been modified over time? Open in a new tab Abbreviations: CFIR, Consolidated Framework for Implementation Research; PRECEDE, Predisposing, Reinforcing, and Enabling Constructs in Educational Diagnosis and Evaluation; PROCEED, Policy, Regulatory, and Organizational Constructs in Educational and Environmental Development; RE-AIM, Reach, Efficacy/Effectiveness, Adoption, Implementation, and Maintenance. Predisposing, Reinforcing, and Enabling Constructs in Educational Diagnosis and Evaluation (PRECEDE) and the Consolidated Framework for Implementation Research (CFIR) are useful frameworks for intervention development. The crux of PRECEDE is that an intervention must be strategically planned before implementation ( Green, 1974 ). It begins with a focus on the desired outcome. Relevant factors impeding the outcome are identified, followed by predisposing, reinforcing, and enabling factors that influence whether change will occur. Finally, policy and administrative elements that affect implementation are considered. The CFIR integrates constructs across several implementation theories to produce a meta-theory that helps guide “what works where and why across multiple contexts” ( Damschroder et al., 2009 ). The five domains of CFIR include (i) intervention characteristics, (ii) outer setting, (iii) inner setting, (iv) individual characteristics, and (v) process of implementation, and each of these areas are further defined in Table 1 ( CFIR Research Team, 2016 ). The CFIR offers the advantage of comprehensiveness without sacrificing pragmatism, and it utilizes standardized terminology that permits intra-study and cross-study comparison. Unlike PRECEDE and CFIR, which are used to evaluate interventions, the Reach, Efficacy/Effectiveness, Adoption, Implementation, and Maintenance (RE-AIM [ Glasgow et al., 1999 ]) and Policy, Regulatory, and Organizational Constructs in Educational and Environmental Development (PROCEED [ Green and Kreuter, 2005 ]) frameworks are frequently used to evaluate outcomes. Table 1 summarizes the elements of both frameworks ( Klesges et al., 2005 ; RE-AIM, 2019 ). Recommendations for the successful application of RE-AIM in real-world settings include monitoring data on each dimension; tracking implementation, costs, and fidelity across settings; and testing adaptations across the dimensions ( Harden et al., 2018 ). PROCEED, an evaluation framework often used after PRECEDE, contains several phases (i.e., implementation, process evaluation, impact evaluation, and outcome evaluation), with an emphasis on active participation by the target population throughout ( Green and Kreuter, 2005 ). Both RE-AIM and PROCEED are highly operational in nature and can be applied to interventions aimed at individuals, organizations, or communities ( Tabak et al., 2012 ). Other implementation science frameworks exist but are either not used as frequently in the literature or combine elements of the frameworks described above, so they are not elaborated in this article. These frameworks include the Dynamic Sustainability Framework ( Chambers et al., 2013 ), which investigates the sustainability of implemented interventions with the presumption that interventions need to be adaptable in order to be sustainable, and the Practical, Robust Implementation and Sustainability Model (PRISM), which expands on the RE-AIM and PRECEDE-PROCEED frameworks and incorporates key elements from other evidence-based medicine models to examine how an intervention interacts with recipients "to influence program adoption, implementation, maintenance, reach, and effectiveness" ( Feldstein and Glasgow, 2008 ). LEVELS OF INTERVENTION Interventions that researchers plan to implement can address stakeholders in different levels of health care. Patient-level interventions aim to influence the individual patient and interpersonal interactions, such as incentivizing lifestyle modifications, educating patients to seek out and maintain preventive care, and facilitating productive social relationships that reinforce healthy behaviors. Provider-level interventions seek to modify behavior at the provider level, most commonly ordering and prescribing patterns. System-level interventions target the operation of health care delivery systems, such as hospitals, clinics, and group-practice organizations, while population-level interventions aim to influence the broader population (community, nation, and region) by encouraging age-appropriate screenings, increasing public awareness of health threats, and modifying public sentiment to discourage unhealthy behaviors. In the following sections, we describe examples of implementation and de-implementation studies in the dermatology literature that are appropriately mapped to each of these levels. We also propose potential implementation studies based on dermatology literature when implementation studies specific to the specialty could not be identified. Patient-level interventions The U.S. Preventive Services Task Force currently recommends that children, adolescents, and young adults with fair skin aged 6 months to 24 years receive counseling regarding the need to minimize exposure to UV radiation in order to reduce the risk of skin cancer ( U.S. Preventive Services Task Force, 2018 ). The U.S. Preventive Services Task Force has graded the strength of the evidence behind this recommendation as level B, indicating that the net benefit is likely moderate to substantial. An implementation study could be designed to increase the uptake of preventive measures to avoid UV radiation exposure by comparing electronic learning modules, take-home printed materials, and reminders delivered by mail, phone, or e-mail on the adoption rates of UV radiation-protective behaviors. The design of the study would depend on the particular context in which implementation occurs and the age group targeted. Provider-level interventions An intervention at the provider level aims to modify provider behavior in order to better align actual practice with current evidence. A range of possible interventions fall within this category, including provider education, the use of order sets, electronic health record reminders, and patient registries. Opportunities abound to design provider-level interventions to improve dermatologic care delivery. For example, current evidence does not support the re-excision of mildly and moderately dysplastic nevi with clear margins ( Fleming et al., 2016 ; Kim et al., 2015 ). After choosing an appropriate framework, an implementation study could be designed at the provider level to reduce the frequency of inappropriate re-excisions. An electronic health record-based notification could alert a physician who enters a diagnosis of a mildly or moderately dysplastic nevus that observation, not excision, is recommended and provide a link to the most recent guidelines. Alternatively, an implementation study could be designed to encourage pathologists to incorporate the recommendation against excision into their final reports, with the knowledge that this message will reach both patients and providers. De-implementation studies can also be performed when certain practices lack clinical or cost-effectiveness. For example, treating patients with terbinafine for suspected onychomycosis is more cost-effective than conducting confirmatory diagnostic testing with potassium hydroxide and/or periodic acid-Schiff ( Mikailov et al., 2016 ). It would cost an estimated $18.2–43.7 million in diagnostic testing to avoid one false-positive case of severe liver injury secondary to terbinafine use. If a clinic wanted to stop confirmatory diagnostic testing for onychomycosis, a de-implementation study can be performed by randomizing clinicians into different intervention groups, and assessing which group adopts the desired clinical practice most. Example interventions include electronic reminders that potassium hydroxide or periodic acid-Schiff testing is unnecessary when these orders are placed, performance feedback on each providers’ diagnostic tests for onychomycosis, and education sessions about practice recommendations. System-level interventions With respect to implementation efforts, an academic medical center in the Netherlands implemented an intervention to prevent contact dermatitis owing to hand washing among health care workers ( Soltanipoor et al., 2019 ). Nineteen hospital wards consisting of over 500 health care workers were randomized to an intervention consisting of electronic dispensers of hand cream and real-time monitoring of cream utilization versus usual care (an educational course on skin protection). The study measured self-reported cream consumption before, during, and after each shift. The proportion of health care workers who used moisturizing cream before their shifts increased in both the intervention and control groups (intervention: 73 of 283 [26%] increased to 83 of 165 [50%]; control: 57 of 217 [26%] increased to 47 of 136 [35%]). During shifts, the intervention group had a 32% increase in the number of health care workers who used moisturizing creams (88 of 285 [31%] to 106 of 168 [63%]) whereas the control group had a 6% increase (67 of 218 [31%] to 49 of 134 [37%]). Overall, the intervention group was 2.27 times more likely to report using hand cream before shifts (odds ratio 2.27, 95% confidence interval 1.29–3.97, P = 0.004) and 3.3 times more likely to report using hand cream during shifts (odds ratio 3.30, 95% confidence interval 1.80–6.06, P < 0.001). Population-level interventions German Statutory Health Insurance launched the first-ever population-wide skin cancer screening program in 2008 to reduce melanoma mortality and improve early detection of skin cancer across Germany ( Kaiser et al., 2018 ). The program offered enrollees aged 35 and older a total body skin exam conducted by a dermatologist or a general practitioner every 2 years at no cost to the enrollee. In contrast to the findings of an earlier pilot study conducted in Northern Germany, the population-wide screening program did not improve melanoma mortality; however, the program increased the diagnosis rate of skin cancer ( Kaiser et al., 2018 ). Currently, the U.S. Preventive Services Task Force does not recommend population-wide skin cancer screening owing to insufficient evidence of its benefits in persons of average risk ( U.S. Preventive Services Task Force, 2016 ). However, certain populations (i.e., people with a family history of melanoma, previous skin cancer, immuno-compromized states, and numerous dysplastic nevi) have an increased risk of skin cancer ( Lindelöf et al., 2000 ; Marcil and Stern, 2000 ). Consequently, a population-wide screening program for these high-risk populations could be designed and implemented, with various options for the scope and scale (local, state-wide, nation-wide, and public or private insurance-based). CALL FOR THE EXPANSION OF IMPLEMENTATION SCIENCE TO DERMATOLOGY Implementation science is a tool to study the final stages of translation once an intervention is supported by strong evidence that it is effective, high-value, and appropriate ( Kitson et al., 2008 ). It is important to note that many guidelines are established based on clinical benefit, not cost-effectiveness. Consequently, implementation research does not necessarily promote value-driven care unless those leading the implementation make resource allocation decisions to reduce low-value care, in which case methods of implementation research can be used for de-implementation of interventions that do not work (i.e., “no” value) or those that work but are costly relative to their clinical benefit (i.e., “low” value) ( Davidson et al., 2017 ). Policy changes can help guide the field of dermatology toward better translation of evidence to practice. As the evidence-base within dermatology strengthens, clinicians, healthcare systems, and policymakers must simultaneously think about how best to implement evidence, which is where the knowledge of implementation science becomes critical to achieving this objective. Although implementation science in dermatology is in its infancy, there are a few studies starting to enter the field; for example, the SKINCARE Trial, a study protocol published in May 2019, is testing the feasibility of implementing an evidenced-based skin care intervention in 20 long-term care facilities in Berlin, Germany ( Kottner et al., 2019 ). Dermatologists interested in obtaining further training in implementation science can use existing resources including National Institutes of Health training events with opportunities for both online and in-person training ( National Institutes of Health–Office of Behavioral and Social Sciences Research, 2018 ); the National Cancer Institute website with resources for training, funding, and collaboration with other implementation researchers ( National Cancer Institute Division of Cancer Control and Population Sciences, 2019a );and the National Cancer Institute’s Training Institute for Dissemination and Implementation Research in Cancer (2019b) . Other academic institutions, most notably the University of North Carolina (2019) and Washington University in St. Louis (2019) , have research arms dedicated to implementation science that offer useful web-based resources and degree- and non-degree–granting programs. In addition, interested clinicians and researchers can partner with established implementation scientists who can help guide the process of accelerating the translation of new treatments and diagnostics into practice and policy. Finally, there must be an expansion of funding opportunities to researchers seeking to conduct implementation studies relating to dermatology. The National Institutes of Health has made a notable effort to support implementation science within its research agenda ( Glasgow et al., 2012 ), and it maintains a database of grant funding opportunities that can be filtered for those relating to implementation ( National Institutes of Health – Office of Disease Prevention, 2019 ). Foundations focused on funding skin-related disease research can follow suit by highlighting implementation science-based projects as an area of need. Academic dermatology journals can increase the impact of implementation study findings by showcasing well-conducted implementation studies and by soliciting invited commentaries to accompany them. CONCLUSION With rising healthcare costs, healthcare systems are increasingly focused on delivering high-value care while minimizing low-value care. Capacity must be developed within the field of dermatology to implement existing innovations and evidence, so that we can be prepared for future discovery. Implementation science provides a systematic approach to identifying and addressing the barriers and facilitators to evidence assimilation and thereby provides a path to achieve durable improvements in dermatologic practice. We provide a guide to help clinicians understand the principles and methods of implementation science and its potential utility in dermatology, with the goal of paving the way for future dermatologic implementation science research. ACKNOWLEDGMENTS We would like to thank Laura Karas for her role in the preparation of this manuscript. This work was supported by the National Institute of Arthritis and Musculoskeletal and Skin Diseases (K24 AR069760 to MA). Abbreviation: RE-AIM Reach, Efficacy/Effectiveness, Adoption, Implementation, and Maintenance