INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2625,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11166523','Dermatology residents'' perspectives on virtual dermatopathology education.','Ngo TB, Niu W, Fang Z, Gold L.','2024','dermatopathological header x40 x100 zooming magnifications perceptive surmise incongruency misinterpreted commute ignited anytime circumvented biopsied incongruency pathpresenter dissuading educative brick streamlines zhide nigms u54 gm104940 lsuhsc khullar e13755 hinshaw stratman shahriari kels marsch espiritu groth hutchens feeser lampros woosley brick sluzevich cappel dicaudo comfere arpa vyas markow granada clindermatol hassell pantanowitz','Abstract Background: Dermatopathology education accounts for 30% of U.S. dermatology residency training. The Covid-19 pandemic expedited implementation of virtual dermatopathology in place of traditional microscopy for resident education. This study examined U.S. dermatology residents’ perceptions of virtual dermatopathology, as research in this area is lacking. Methods: An anonymous, confidential, IRB-approved survey was electronically distributed to U.S. dermatology residents consisting of 16 questions comparing attitudes towards virtual and traditional dermatopathology education. Responses were n = 59. Statistical analysis was performed using SAS software. Results: Participants believe virtual imaging is superior to conventional microscopy in schedule flexibility (96.6% vs 1.7%, p<0.0001), lecture convenience (94.8% vs 0.0%, p<0.0001), personal review (96.6% vs 0.0%, p<0.0001), cost effectiveness (64.4% vs 6.8%, p<0.0001), and board exam preparation (52.5% vs 16.9%, p=0.0005). Conventional microscopy was favored for image quality (50.8% vs 25.4%, p=0.0127) and overall utility (50.8% vs 27.1%, p=0.0195). Conclusions: Our study supports virtual dermatopathology utilization as a valuable tool in dermatology residency training. Also it is shown that conventional microscopy training continues to play a key role. Further studies should examine whether, if ever, virtual dermatopathology could gradually replace conventional microscopy with the advent of newer and more powerful digital and scanning technology. Keywords: virtual dermatopathology, dermatology residents, resident perspectives, dermatopathology training Introduction: Dermatopathology training is an integral part of a dermatology resident’s postgraduate curriculum. 1 2 Since 2018, all U.S. dermatology residents are required to complete the American Board of Dermatology’s (ABD) staged certification pathway to demonstrate clinical competency before advancing in training. This certification pathway is a three-staged examination series consisting of a BASIC exam, 4 CORE exam modules, and an APPLIED exam. These are taken at various points in residency training, depending on post-grad year (PGY). 2 The CORE exams are designed for second- and third-year dermatology residents, and include modules in the 4 major clinical areas of dermatology as determined by the ABD—medicine, pediatrics, surgery, and dermatopathology. 2 Among the 52 dermatology residency programs that responded to a 2009 survey from the Association of Professors of Dermatology, it was found that approximately 30% of didactic education in dermatology residency is dedicated to dermatopathology education. 3 Educating dermatology residents in the fundamentals of dermatopathology helps bridge the gap between clinical and laboratory medicine, allowing for improved diagnostic ability and higher-quality patient care. 3 In recent years there has been a push to increase accessibility in dermatopathological education through virtual adaptation, and this process was accelerated by the COVID-19 pandemic. Traditional dermatopathology education involved conventional microscopy, in which students attended faculty-led seminars discussing prepared glass slides, typically on a 5- or 10-header microscope. 1 Using still images during didactics means that residents can only view that content during a specific presentation or lecture, resulting in low information accessibility and less self-directed learning. It also restricts the number of people who can view images at any given time, which limits collaborative efforts. 1 In 2017, whole slide imaging was first approved by the US Food and Drug Administration for digital pathology. This imaging method involves scanning glass slides at a magnification of x40 (or x100, in cases were microorganisms need to be visualized), and constructing a composite whole-slide virtual image. 1 The resulting digital slides are then uploaded to a cloud server in which they are categorized and annotated for user access. 4 These digital slides allow for zooming in and out at different magnifications without compromising image quality. 5 The unforeseen necessity of social distancing that accompanied the COVID-19 pandemic made traditional pathology teaching methods less ideal, so this method of virtual dermatopathology imaging quickly gained popularity. Recent literature describes numerous benefits and drawbacks of virtual dermatopathology implementation, but no study currently exists that specifically explores dermatology residents’ opinions on virtual versus conventional dermatopathology training. This study aimed to examine U.S. dermatology residents’ perceptions of virtual dermatopathology education, including whether they find it to be an effective form of instruction. Materials and Methods: An anonymous, confidential survey ( Appendix ) was approved by our institutional review board and electronically distributed to dermatology program coordinators in North America and the Association of Professors of Dermatology. The survey was constructed using RedCap, and consisted of a series of 16 short questions comparing attitudes towards virtual and traditional dermatopathology education. It was then forwarded to current dermatology residents via email with a link to the RedCap platform. Written informed consent was obtained from the participants prior to the start of the survey. Participants who did not have experience in both conventional microscopy and virtual dermatopathology during residency or medical school were excluded. This exclusion criteria was mentioned in the email as well as at the beginning of the survey. The emails of those who completed the survey were entered into a drawing to win a new copy of Self-Assessment in Dermatology: Questions and Answers (Elsevier ). Total survey responses was n = 59 ( Table I ). At the time the survey was sent out, there were a total of 1503 residents training at the programs the surveys were emailed to, leading to a response rate of 3.9% (59/1503). Table I: Study Population and Characteristics Variable Characteristics Frequency (N=59) Percent Age 20-30 35 59.3 30-40 23 39.0 40-50 1 1.7 Gender Male 20 33.9 Female 39 66.1 Year of training * PGY-2 24 41.4 PGY-3 19 32.8 PGY-4 15 25.9 Planning to pursue dermatopathology fellowship Yes 4 6.8 No 46 78.0 Undecided 9 15.3 Open in a new tab * One resident did not answer this question. Participant age breakdown was as follows: 59.3% were 20-30 years old, 39.0% were 30-40 years old, and 1.7% were 40-50 years old ( Figure 1 ). Of the 59 participants, 33.9% were male and 66.1% were female ( Figure 2 ). 41.4% of the participants were PGY-2, 32.8% were PGY-3, and 25.9% were PGY-4 ( Figure 3 ). 6.8% of the participants were planning to pursue a dermatopathology fellowship, 78% of participants were not, and the remaining 15.3% were undecided ( Figure 4 ). All analyses were performed using SAS software (version 9.4, Cary, North Carolina). Survey responses were analyzed using a Chi-squared goodness-of-fit test. An exact Chi-squared test was implemented for further interpretation of specific items. Figure 1: Open in a new tab Survey participants demographics by age Figure 2: Open in a new tab Survey participants demographics by sex Figure 3: Open in a new tab Survey participants demographics by PGY level Figure 4: Open in a new tab Survey participants plans to pursue dermatopathology fellowship Statistical Analysis For the first 12 questions in the survey, we estimate the percentages of residents’ opinions on the training tools, “Conventional microscope teaching,” “No significant difference,” and “Virtual dermatopathology teaching,” separately. Percentages of responses to satisfaction levels of using virtual slide images on the dermatology board exam instead of traditional microscopy were obtained. The responses to increased use of virtual pathology education in dermatology resident training in the post-COVID era compared to pre-COVID era were also obtained. The likelihood ratio goodness-of-fit test was employed to test the equality of the proportions in each survey question. Chi square test was used for association studies between resident’s PGY level and his/her response to survey question, [and] between resident’s plan on dermatopathology fellowship and his/her response to survey question. All analyses were carried out in SAS Version 9.4 (Cary, North Carolina, USA). Results: Of the 59 dermatology residents who participated in this survey, 39.0% reported that they find virtual dermatopathology easier to use as a learning tool compared to conventional microscopy (33.9%), and 27.1% of respondents find no difference (ND) between the two methods. The difference in these percentages is not statistically significant (p=0.5341). Regarding image quality, residents favored conventional microscopy over virtual slides (50.8% vs 25.4%, p=0.0127), with 23.7% selecting ND. There was no significant discrepancy in perceived diagnostic accuracy between the 2 teaching methods (27.6% vs 22.4%, p = 0.5775), with 50% of residents stating there is ND. More (50.8%) respondents believe that conventional microscopy data are easier to communicate to colleagues and peers during lecture than virtual slide data (37.3%), with 11.9% reporting ND. While the relative proportions of all 3 choices were significant (p=0.001), the difference between conventional and virtual ease of communication was not (p=0.2673). Participants overwhelmingly reported that virtual dermatopathology offers more schedule flexibility (96.6% vs 1.7%, p<0.0001) than conventional microscopy. Cost-effectiveness was conceptualized in terms of residents’ perceptive of the overall costs of virtual dermatopathology implementation (e.g., slide scanning, software licensing, etc.). Participants in this study felt that virtual dermatopathology is more cost-efficient than traditional microscopy (64.4% vs 6.8%, p<0.0001). This is in direct opposition to current literature, which consistently sites cost as one of the most significant barriers to virtual pathology utilization. We surmise that this incongruency may be due to information gap, as many residents may not yet have enough clinical experience to understand the intricacies and magnitude of healthcare costs. It is also possible that this question could have been misinterpreted such that respondents considered cost at the individual level rather than at the institutional level (e.g., virtual dermatopathology may decrease need for residents to commute to lectures). Convenience was analyzed in terms of which lecture format is easier to attend, as well as which format is easier to review on one’s own time. No (0) residents thought that conventional microscopy lectures were superior to those involving virtual slides for either of these measures of convenience. Significantly more respondents selected “virtual microscopy” over “ND” regarding convenience of lecture attendance (94.8% vs 5.2%, p<0.0001) and convenience of personal review (96.6% vs 3.4%, p<0.0001). There was no significant difference in capacity for knowledge or graphic memory retention between participants who chose conventional microscopy, virtual slides, or ND (39% vs 30.5% vs 30.5%, p = 0.6546). When asked which form of training is more helpful in preparing for the dermatology board exam, significantly more residents chose virtual education over conventional (52.5% vs 16.9%, p=0.0005). Most survey participants believe that conventional microscopy is superior to virtual dermatopathology in terms of overall utility (50.8% vs 27.1%), and this was significant at p=0.0195. Comprehensive statistical data for the above are summarized in Table II . Table II: Current U.S. Dermatology Residents’ Perceptions of Conventional Microscopy vs Virtual Dermatopathology in Dermatology Residency Training (Survey Responses for Questions 1-12): Variable Conventional Virtual No difference (ND) Chi-squared p-value Ease of use as a learning tool Frequency 20 23 16 Among all 3: p=0.5341 Percent 33.9 39.0 27.1 Image quality Frequency 30 15 14 Among all 3: p=0.0168* Percent 50.8 25.4 23.7 Conventional vs virtual: p=0.0127* Diagnostic accuracy Frequency 16 13 30 Among all 3: p=0.0237* Percent 27.6 22.4 50.0 Conventional vs virtual: p=0.5775 Ease of result communication during lecture Frequency 30 22 7 Among all 3: p=0.001* Percent 50.8 37.3 11.9 Conventional vs virtual: p=0.2673 Schedule flexibility Frequency 1 57 1 Among all 3: p<0.0001* Percent 1.7 96.6 1.7 Conventional vs virtual: p<0.0001* Convenience (to attend) Frequency 0 56 3 ND vs virtual: p<0.0001* Percent 0.0 94.8 5.2 Cost efficiency Frequency 4 38 17 Among all 3: p<0.0001* Percent 6.8 64.4 28.8 Conventional vs virtual: p< 0.0001* Convenience (to review on own time) Frequency 0 57 2 ND vs virtual: p<0.0001* Percent 0.0 96.6 3.4 Knowledge retention Frequency 23 18 18 Among all 3: p=0.8546 Percent 39.0 30.5 30.5 Graphic memory retention Frequency 20 12 27 Among all 3: p=0.057 Percent 33.9 20.3 45.8 Board exam helpfulness Frequency 10 31 18 Among all 3: p=0.0033* Percent 16.9 52.5 30.5 Conventional vs virtual: p=0.0005* Overall utility Frequency 30 16 13 Among all 3: p=0.0152* Percent 50.8 27.1 22.0 Conventional vs virtual: p=0.0195* Open in a new tab Participants were also asked to rate their satisfaction levels with the replacement of traditional microscopy with virtual slide images on the dermatology board exam. A Likert-type scale was used to measure responses, which were as follows: 1.7% very dissatisfied, 1.7% somewhat dissatisfied, 42.4% neutral, 33.9% somewhat satisfied, and 20.3% very satisfied. Statistical analysis revealed that there are significant differences among the proportions of all five choices (p<0.0001), and the number of respondents who are either somewhat satisfied or very satisfied with this change is significantly greater than those who are somewhat dissatisfied or very dissatisfied (p<0.0001). The vast majority of survey participants (84.5%) reported that they expect increased use of virtual pathology education in dermatology resident training in the post-Covid era compared to the pre-Covid era. Only 1.7% do not anticipate seeing this trend, and 13.8% are unsure. There is significant difference among the proportions of all three choices (p<0.0001), and the number of those expecting increased utilization of virtual teaching post-Covid is significantly greater than those who are not (p<0.0001). Expanding upon this outside of the context of Covid, respondents were asked which trend they expect to see with virtual dermatopathology teaching in future residency training. 96.6% said it will likely increase, 0.0% said it will likely decrease, and 3.4% said it will stay at the same level. The proportion of residents who expect an increasing trend is significantly greater than those who expect no change (p<0.0001). Finally, participants overwhelmingly agree that overall, virtual dermatopathology teaching is useful in dermatopathology training (yes 96.6% vs no 3.4%, p < 0.0001). Further statistical analysis was performed to investigate whether level of residency training (PGY) or having a plan to pursue dermatopathology fellowship impacted perceptions. No significant associations were found between PGY level and survey responses, but there is mild evidence (p<0.1) supporting an association between plans to pursue dermatopathology fellowship with perceptions of knowledge retention and overall utility of the two learning platforms. Discussion: The implementation of virtual dermatopathology education as a supplement or possible replacement for traditional microscopy during the Covid-19 pandemic has ignited conversations surrounding the future of dermatology residency training. There is existing literature arguing both for and against virtual and traditional microscopy education, but research regarding resident perceptions of these teaching methods is lacking. Our study addressed this disconnect, with an ultimate goal of assisting dermatology program directors in effectively updating residency training curricula without compromising diagnostic accuracy, overall efficiency, and other crucial factors. Dermatology residents who chose to participate in the study ranged from ages 20-50 years, and were either PGY-2, PGY-3, or PGY-4. Respondents disclosed their gender and whether or not they had future plans to pursue a dermatopathology fellowship. They were then asked to select either “virtual slide dermatopathology,” “conventional microscopy,” or “ND,” depending on which method they believe to be superior on the basis of twelve distinct variables ( Table II ). Prior research shows that virtual dermatopathology training increases convenience and efficiency on multiple levels. The ability to access slides anytime, anywhere, on any mobile device effectively eliminates the problem of student volume exceeding microscope availability at one time at a given institution. 1 Storage of digital images instead of traditional glass slides is vastly more practical in terms of both specimen quality and physical space. The possibility of glass slides breaking or being otherwise damaged is circumvented, which is especially crucial when examining rare conditions that are infrequently encountered and biopsied. 1 Additionally, decreasing the need for physical space to store glass slides is beneficial for institutions and labs that may be lacking in this area. 1 Unsurprisingly, results of this study reveal that dermatology residents believe that virtual slide imaging is superior to conventional microscopy in terms of schedule flexibility (96.6% vs 1.7%, p<0.0001), convenience for attending lecture (94.8% vs 0.0%, p<0.0001), and convenience for personal review (96.6% vs 0.0%, p<0.0001). These findings suggesting increased practicality and accessibility of digital dermatopathology are in accordance with multiple prior studies. 3 , 5 , 6 While the potential benefits of virtual dermatopathology are significant, the process of creating virtual slides is time-intensive, and purchasing an adequate slide scanner is expensive. Despite different pricing models for healthcare software licensing, implementation costs can be a massive financial burden, especially for smaller institutions. 7 The time necessary for slide preparation ranges from 20-30 minutes per slide, and appropriate preparation may require technicians and software engineers. 1 Interestingly, residents who participated in our study also felt that whole slide imaging is more cost-effective (64.4% vs 6.8%, p<0.0001). Explanation for this incongruency may include the use of an established database for teaching cases (eg, Pathpresenter), at the respective resident’s institution, which would have little marginal cost, or possibly an information gap about the comprehensive cost of virtual dermatopathology as mentioned previously. The residents also found that virtual dermatopathology more helpful for dermatology board exam preparation (52.5% vs 16.9%, p=0.0005) than traditional glass-slide microscopy. While there is some research supporting the notion that virtual pathology may be more affordable than the conventional glass-slide method, 6 , 8 many studies indicate that the high cost of virtual slide imaging equipment may be a dissuading factor for certain programs in making digital dermatopathology standard practice. 1 , 5 ,9499 In terms of usefulness during preparation for the dermatology board exam, a 2008 randomized comparison among dermatology and pathology residents found that only 44% of respondents favored virtual microscopy for testing purposes. 6 Similarly, a 2021 survey of 261 pathology trainees, faculty, and program directors revealed that the advent of digital pathology has had a negative impact upon quality and effectiveness of resident education. 10 In our study, more than half of the dermatology residents favored virtual over conventional dermatopathology teaching for this purpose. This may be, in part, due to the previously mentioned advantages of convenience and access with whole-slide imaging. We believe this represents a convincing argument in favor of virtual pathology training. The use of virtual dermatopathology in dermatology resident training specifically has also raised questions regarding interpretation accuracy and effect on workflow. 10 To address these concerns, studies have been conducted to analyze differences in acceptability, diagnostic accuracy, and educative efficacy between virtual dermatopathology and conventional dermatopathology. 1 In study of 499 dermatopathology cases, Kent et al. found that virtually-rendered slide images and traditional glass-slide microscopy were equivalent in diagnostic accuracy. 10 A randomized controlled trial involving a cohort of dermatology and pathology residents at 14 different training institutions performed by Koch et al reported similar findings. The residents were asked to examine randomized combinations of 20 virtual and glass slide images and provide their diagnostic impressions. Diagnostic validity was similar between the two groups, and 79% of residents in the study supported the belief that virtual microscopy is helpful in dermatopathology training. 6 Brick et al found that those with prior experience and training in virtual microscopy are likely to perform better with digital slides 11 In our study, conventional microscopy was favored over digital imaging for image quality (50.8% vs 25.4%, p=0.0127) in our study, which did not necessarily align with findings from the literature. Similar surveys conducted at other institutions found that residents perceived no significant difference in image quality between the two methods. 3 , 7 In one randomized study involving both dermatology and pathology residents, there were some quality concerns for whole slide imaging, but subsequent diagnostic accuracy was unaffected. 6 It is also important to note that virtual slide image quality is largely dependent upon the caliber of available scanning equipment at a given institution, and may be impacted by external factors such as computer processing speed, screen resolution, and human operator error. 9 , 10 50.0% of our survey respondents perceive ND in diagnostic accuracy between the two techniques (p=0.0237), and this was in agreement with various prior publications. 6 , 10 One study, however, found that pathologists’ average percentage of correctly identified features was higher on digital media than glass media. 9 Additional studies are needed to further evaluate diagnostic accuracy, and to provide continued insight into the functionality of whole slide imaging in both academic and clinical settings. Our study also found ND in potential for graphic memory retention, but this was not significant. Our participants found conventional microscopy to be more useful overall in dermatopathology training compared to virtual slide education (50.8% vs 27.1%, p=0.0195). While this survey question is intentionally vague, the responses undoubtedly support continued usage of traditional dermatopathology in some form, at least until there is evidence to suggest otherwise. Conventional microscopy was also felt to be superior to digital imaging in capacity for knowledge retention and ease of result communication during lecture, but these associations were not statistically significant. This study is limited by a small sample size of 59 dermatology residents with a response rate of 3.9% (59/1503). We therefore recognize probable selection bias, since we cannot say with certainty that our results reflect the perceptions of all dermatology residents currently training in the U.S. We therefore recognize probable selection bias, since we cannot say with certainty that our results reflect the perceptions of all dermatology residents currently training in the U.S. To note as well, because the emails were sent to the program coordinators to be distributed to their residents, and not to the residents directly, we cannot truly assess how many residents actually received the surveys and therefore the response rate may differ. While only one survey question involved a Likert-type response scale, we must also acknowledge the possibility of extreme response bias and neutral responding bias for this item. We recognize that implementing more Likert-type items in our survey may have allowed for more nuanced responses, but extreme response bias and neutral responding bias would likely have been more significant. Despite these limitations, our study supports both the continued use of traditional microscopy and virtual imaging in dermatology residency training programs. Further studies are needed to examine when, if ever, whole slide digital imaging should replace conventional teaching methods. It is clear, however, that adapting dermatopathology training to the virtual sphere removes access barriers for trainees and streamlines much of the collaborative process. Studies such as this one that elucidate resident perceptions of various teaching methodologies are not only important, but necessary to most effectively shape dermatology residency program curricula and thus guide the future leaders of the field. Acknowledgements: Everyone that contributed to this manuscript meets criteria for authorship, as listed above. Zhide Fang was supported in part by NIH/NIGMS with grant U54 GM104940. Appendix: Survey Questions Asked of Dermatology Residents to Assess Perceptions of Virtual Dermatopathology Training vs Traditional Microscopy: Which format do you find easier to use as a learning tool? Conventional microscope teaching Virtual dermatopathology teaching No significant difference Which format has better slide image quality? Conventional microscope teaching Virtual dermatopathology teaching No significant difference Which format provides more diagnostic information that helps with diagnostic accuracy? Conventional microscope teaching Virtual dermatopathology teaching No significant difference Which format is easier to communicate with faculty and peers during lecture? Conventional microscope teaching Virtual dermatopathology teaching No significant difference Which format offers more flexibility to your schedule? Conventional microscope teaching Virtual dermatopathology teaching No significant difference Which format offers more convenience to attend? Conventional microscope teaching Virtual dermatopathology teaching No significant difference Which format is more cost-effective to benefit most residents for maximum learning? Conventional microscope teaching Virtual dermatopathology teaching No significant difference Which format is easier to review on your own time? Conventional microscope teaching Virtual dermatopathology teaching No significant difference Which format is more efficient for knowledge retention? Conventional microscope teaching Virtual dermatopathology teaching No significant difference Which format is more efficient to retain dermatopathology graphic memory/ Conventional microscope teaching Virtual dermatopathology teaching No significant difference Which format do you consider more helpful for the preparation of the dermatology board exam? Conventional microscope teaching Virtual dermatopathology teaching No significant difference Which format do you consider more useful for dermatopathology training overall? Conventional microscope teaching Virtual dermatopathology teaching No significant difference What is your satisfaction level with the newly adopted digital slides format that has replaced traditional microscopic format as a testing tool for the dermatology board exam? Very dissatisfied Somewhat dissatisfied Neutral Somewhat satisfied Very satisfied Compared to the pre-Covid era, do you see a trend of increased use of virtual dermatopathology teaching in the dermatology resident training program in the post-Covid era? Yes No Not sure What trend do you expect to see with the usage of virtual dermatopathology teaching in future residency training? Will likely increase Will likely decrease Will be at the same level Overall, do you consider virtual dermatopathology teaching useful in your dermatopathology training? Yes No Footnotes Conflicts of Interest: None of the authors have any conflicts of interest to disclose. Ethical statement: This human study was approved by LSUHSC New Orleans Institutional Review Board. Written informed consent was obtained from all individual participants prior to participating. References: 1. Khullar G, Chandra M. Virtual dermatopathology: A potential educational tool during COVID-19 pandemic. Dermatol Ther. 2020;33(4):e13755. doi: 10.1111/dth.13755 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Hinshaw M, Hsu P, Lee LY, Stratman E. The current state of dermatopathology education: a survey of the association of professors of dermatology. J Cutan Pathol. 2009;36(6):620–628. doi: 10.1111/j.1600-0560.2008.01128.x [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Shahriari N, Grant-Kels J, Murphy MJ. Dermatopathology education in the era of modern technology. J Cutan Pathol. 2017;44(9):763–771. doi: 10.1111/cup.12980 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Marsch AF, Espiritu B, Groth J, Hutchens KA. The effectiveness of annotated (vs. non-annotated) digital pathology slides as a teaching tool during dermatology and pathology residencies. J Cutan Pathol. 2014;41(6):513–518. doi: 10.1111/cup.12328 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Kent MN, Olsen TG, Feeser TA, et al. Diagnostic accuracy of virtual pathology vs traditional microscopy in a large dermatopathology study. JAMA Dermatol. 2017;153(12):1285–1291. doi: 10.1001/jamadermatol.2017.3284 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Koch LH, Lampros JN, Delong LK, Chen SC, Woosley JT, Hood AF. Randomized comparison of virtual microscopy and traditional glass microscopy in diagnostic accuracy among dermatology and pathology residents. Hum Pathol. 2009;40(5):662–667. doi: 10.1016/j.humpath.2008.10.009 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Brick KE, Sluzevich JC, Cappel MA, DiCaudo DJ, Comfere NI, Wieland CN. Comparison of virtual microscopy and glass slide microscopy among dermatology residents during a simulated in-training examination. J Cutan Pathol. 2013;40(9):807–811. doi: 10.1111/cup.12189 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Chin KE, Kwon D, Gan Q, et al. Transition from a standard to a hybrid on-site and remote anatomic pathology training model during the coronavirus disease 2019 (COVID-19) pandemic. Arch Pathol Lab Med. 2021;145(1):22–31. doi: 10.5858/arpa.2020-0467-SA [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Vyas NS, Markow M, Prieto-Granada C, et al. Comparing whole slide digital images versus traditional glass slides in the detection of common microscopic features seen in dermatitis. J Pathol Inform. 2016;7:30.doi: 10.4103/2153-3539.186909 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Mahmood MN. Dermatopathology resident training and education during the COVID-19 pandemic: challenges faced and lessons learned. Clin Dermatol. 2021;39(5):907–910. doi: 10.1016/j.clindermatol.2021.08.016 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Hassell LA, Peterson J, Pantanowitz L. Pushed across the digital divide: COVID-19 accelerated pathology training onto a new digital learning curve. Acad Pathol. 2021;8:2374289521994240. doi: 10.1177/2374289521994240 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',7,'2025-10-14 06:40:51','2025-10-14 06:40:51');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2626,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8757299','Towards gender equity in artificial intelligence and machine learning applications in dermatology.','Lee MS, Guo LN, Nambudiri VE.','2022','widened lentiginous sclerosus sclerosus frenulum blanching sclerosus fissures sclerosus balanitis centrifugal ccca discoid dle ancestries balanitis lentiginous disregarding dampened msl lng esteva kuprel novoa zakhem fakhoury motosko haenssle schneiderbauer canfield ivanova renaud hairy e14811 manohar achar manohar dhane maity mungle shrivastava londhe sonawane suri larrazabal milone ferrante cirillo catuara solarz morey pandeya whiteman jakowatz meyskens ijerph ijerph16060908 ragnarsson olding kanter lewensohn lagerlof ringborg latini cota orsini cristaudo tedesco sclerosus postepy alergol ojbd nieva elhadad arxiv201106100 bieber pomeranz oddis janoski casterline medsger allegheny endean loussouarn panhard collaudin rawadi genain ejd trichoscopic johannet sawyers tkachenko mostaghimi kharazmi sontag hamann buolamwini gebru shades v81 buolamwini18a','Abstract There has been increased excitement around the use of machine learning (ML) and artificial intelligence (AI) in dermatology for the diagnosis of skin cancers and assessment of other dermatologic conditions. As these technologies continue to expand, it is essential to ensure they do not create or widen sex- and gender-based disparities in care. While desirable bias may result from the explicit inclusion of sex or gender in diagnostic criteria of diseases with gender-based differences, undesirable biases can result from usage of datasets with an underrepresentation of certain groups. We believe that sex and gender differences should be taken into consideration in ML/AI algorithms in dermatology because there are important differences in the epidemiology and clinical presentation of dermatologic conditions including skin cancers, sex-specific cancers, and autoimmune conditions. We present recommendations for ensuring sex and gender equity in the development of ML/AI tools in dermatology to increase desirable bias and avoid undesirable bias. Keywords: artificial intelligence, machine learning, gender, equity, dermatology, disparities Machine learning and artificial intelligence have broad applications in healthcare, with the potential to improve diagnosis and management of disease and increase access to care. This excitement for machine learning (ML) and artificial intelligence (AI) applications also extends to the field of dermatology. The largest body of work relates to AI applications for diagnostic support of assessing lesions for skin cancers, including using deep learning convolutional neural networks to classify images of skin lesions as melanoma or other skin cancers. 1–3 Early evidence has demonstrated performance of these technologies on-par with dermatologists for skin cancer classification. 1–3 AI has also been applied to the diagnosis of hair loss, using microscopy and image analysis to perform hair density measurements. 4 , 5 Finally, additional applications include the characterization of other cutaneous conditions such as chronic wounds 6 , 7 and autoimmune inflammatory dermatoses, such as psoriasis. 8 As dermatologic applications of AI broaden and are incorporated into clinical care, especially for diagnostic support, it is essential to consider the role of sex and gender equity to ensure that uptake of these new technologies does not result in new or widened disparities in clinical care. Within dermatology, there has been much discussion on the lack of racial diversity in ML algorithms as a potential source of bias that can perpetuate health disparities for skin of color. 9 Similarly, there is potential for bias if certain sexes or genders are excluded from datasets used to train and develop machine learning algorithms or if sex or gender-based differences in clinical presentation are not adequately accounted for. More broadly, AI systems trained predominately on male subjects perform worse when tested on female subjects. 10 However, there is limited literature on considerations of sex or gender and AI in dermatology. Given the threats and vulnerabilities of AI to bias, increased and intentional consideration of sex and gender is critical to ensure equity in ML and AI applications in dermatology. We will focus our discussion on the applications of AI in dermatology diagnostic support for cutaneous malignancies, autoimmune inflammatory conditions, and alopecia. In considering bias in AI, 2 types of biases have been described in ML literature: desirable and undesirable biases. 11 Desirable biases result from accounting for differences between groups to allow for more accurate and tailored diagnostic and treatment plans, whereas undesirable biases occur when algorithms developed based on lack of adequate evidence or based on skewed evidence result in discrimination. 11 For sex and gender, an example of a desirable bias is the inclusion of sex in diagnostic criteria of diseases with sex-based differences, whereas usage of datasets with an underrepresentation of certain genders exemplifies undesirable biases. There are several reasons sex and gender differences should be taken into account in ML algorithms in dermatology in particular. First, there are important sex and gender-based differences in skin diseases that can impact AI performance, including in the diagnosis of skin cancers. For example, melanomas in females occur more frequently on the hip and lower extremities compared to males. 12 , 13 Sex-specific cancers, such as vulvar neoplasms, also present differently. The majority of vulvar melanomas are mucosal lentiginous or nodular subtypes, while the superficial spreading subtype is much less common. 14 These subtypes all have different gross and histologic features, and the distribution of subtypes in vulvar melanoma differs vastly from melanomas found on other parts of the body. Similarly, genital lichen sclerosus, a chronic disease that can progress to squamous cell carcinoma, has a different clinical presentation among men and women. 15 Early lichen sclerosus may be characterized by color alterations and a short frenulum in males, in contrast to circumscribed blanching and mucosal fragility in females. Later stages of lichen sclerosus also present with stable fissures in different anatomical areas in both sexes. Women are often diagnosed with lichen sclerosus later in life and thus receive treatment later, highlighting the diagnostic challenges and differences by sex. 15 This is consistent with Sun et al’s large-scale fairness analyses of diverse health outcomes, which found that women consistently experience longer time to diagnosis than men. 16 Therefore, algorithms that are not trained and developed using lesions from a wide variety of locations or a diversity of patients may be less accurate in the diagnosis of these dermatologic conditions for particular sexes. This undesirable bias may result in delayed diagnosis of skin cancer and worsening outcomes due to differential performance based on sex and gender, especially for conditions like vulvar cancer, which already result in significant morbidity and mortality due to diagnostic challenges. 17 Furthermore, there are sex-based differences in cutaneous disease prevalence, particularly for autoimmune conditions and connective tissues diseases such as lupus and scleroderma, which occur much more frequently in women. 18 , 19 Though most AI research has been focused on skin cancer diagnosis and classification, applications are also beginning to expand to the classification of inflammatory conditions. 8 To broaden AI applications for the diagnosis of inflammatory dermatoses, considering sex and gender explicitly may represent a desirable bias. Specifically, it may be particularly helpful for informing pretest probability to improve algorithm accuracy and performance. For conditions that exclusively affect certain sexes, such as Bartholin gland cysts in females or balanitis in males, the importance of explicit consideration of sex is even more essential. Inclusion of marginalized populations is especially important given the intersectionality between skin tone—often used as a proxy for race—and gender in numerous dermatologic conditions. For example, many autoimmune causes of scarring alopecia including central centrifugal cicatricial alopecia (CCCA) and discoid lupus erythematosus (DLE) disproportionately affect women of color, necessitating adequate representation. Furthermore, women often experience more severe quality of life impairment from alopecia. 20 It is thus critical to ensure that AI and ML efforts utilize images from patients of diverse skin types and genders for such conditions. To illustrate, there have been recent deep learning-based systems proposed for hair loss diagnosis using convolutional neural networks to detect hair follicles density. 4 , 5 However, studies have shown that hair density differs by ancestry, as Black hair may have lower density and slower growth than Caucasian hair, 21 , 22 and gender, as males have slightly lower hair density than females. 21 These race and sex-based differences in conditions disproportionately affecting women of color underscore the importance of including images from diverse ancestries and genders. We have compiled several recommendations for ensuring sex and gender equity in the development of AI and ML tools in dermatology to increase desirable bias and avoid undesirable bias ( Table 1 ). First, to increase desirable bias, sex and gender-related information should be collected with images, incorporated into patient metadata, and reported when creating algorithms for skin cancer and dermatoses. In fact, studies have shown that integrating clinical metadata including patient sex alongside dermoscopic images generates more accurate clinical diagnoses than dermoscopy alone. 3 , 26 Furthermore, sex and gender composition is not routinely reported or considered in dermatologic AI research, which could mask any underlying biases in the study population. Table 1. Examples of desirable and undesirable biases relevant to dermatologic disease with recommendations for AI/ML algorithms Desirable or undesirable bias Type of bias Dermatologic example Recommendations Desirable Sex-based differences in disease presentations Melanomas in females occur more frequently on the hip and lower extremities compared to males Subtypes of melanomas affecting the vulva differ from subtypes found on other parts of the body Some conditions exclusively affect certain sexes, such as Bartholin gland cysts in females or balanitis in males Autoimmune and connective tissues diseases occur more frequently in women Incorporate sex and gender into patient metadata when creating algorithms Report patient demographics of training and testing datasets Demonstrate fair performance across the spectrum of sexes and genders Sex and gender-based differences intersect with other patient demographics Black patients are more likely to have acral lentiginous melanoma 23 Higher proportion of non-White patients have mucosal melanoma compared to white patients 23 Gay and bisexual men reported 25%–46% higher odds of skin cancer diagnosis compared with matched heterosexual men 24 Account for intersectionality of patient factors, such as race, sexuality, and gender, in algorithms for increased accuracy Ensure adequate representation of gender and racial minorities Demonstrate accuracy of performance for specific, marginalized groups Undesirable Inadequate representation of all sexes and genders in ML/AI datasets Lack of reporting in the literature on sex and gender representation in ML/AI datasets in dermatology Increase reporting of sex and gender distributions for ML/AI datasets Ensure adequate representation of all sexes and genders in datasets Unknown sex and gender-based differences in dermatologic conditions Mixed evidence whether gender minorities have different incidence rates of skin cancer than cis-gender patients 25 Promote future research on differences in dermatologic conditions in all genders, including transgender, nonbinary, and other gender-diverse patients Open in a new tab Accordingly, we also advocate for adequate representation of all genders in artificial intelligence and machine learning data and training sets. The central importance of the representativeness of the data itself in promoting fairness in machine learning diagnostic applications has been recognized more broadly in healthcare. For example, Chen et al proposed that fairness of predictions should be addressed through data collection rather than model constraints in order to minimize discrimination and maintain accuracy. 27 Not only must algorithms be trained on diverse data sets, but it is also critical to intentionally examine and report evidence of no difference in performance of the model based on sex or gender in the test set. This can only be accomplished if sex and gender information is routinely collected and reported, underscoring the importance of this transparency. It is also essential to consider differences in dermatologic conditions of all genders, including transgender, nonbinary, and other gender-diverse patient populations. These patients have unique clinical concerns, including hormone use (which can cause acne and hair disorders), and increased risk of squamous cell carcinoma of neo genitalia from patients who have undergone gender-affirming surgeries. 28 However, these patient populations have historically been omitted from the medical literature, representing a knowledge gap that may perpetuate unknown, undesirable biases in machine learning applications. Thus, further research is needed to understand dermatologic considerations of gender-diverse patients. Finally, beyond gender, algorithm developers should take into account the intersectionality of patient factors including race, sexuality, and gender for more accurate and tailored recommendations. In nonmedical applications, the consequences of disregarding issues of intersectionality manifest in examples such as lower accuracy of facial recognition software in the recognition of women of color in particular. 29 For dermatology specifically, as discussed above, there are gender and ancestral differences in hair which merit consideration by AI developers interested in creating diagnostic technologies for alopecia. Advances in ML and AI are poised to transform the delivery of dermatologic care, but the potential utility may be dampened without recognizing and addressing issues pertaining to diversity in all forms, including sex and gender. To ensure all patients can benefit from the promises of these novel technologies, adequate consideration and representation of all sexes and genders is critically important. FUNDING There is no funding source for this work. AUTHOR CONTRIBUTIONS Authors MSL, LNG, and VEN all made substantial contributions to the conception and design, drafting, and editing of the manuscript. DATA AVAILABILITY STATEMENT No new data were generated or analyzed in support of this research. CONFLICT OF INTEREST STATEMENT None declared. REFERENCES 1. Esteva A, Kuprel B, Novoa RA, et al. Dermatologist-level classification of skin cancer with deep neural networks. Nature 2017; 542 (7639): 115–8. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Zakhem GA, Fakhoury JW, Motosko CC, Ho RS. Characterizing the role of dermatologists in developing artificial intelligence for assessment of skin cancer: a systematic review. J Am Acad Dermatol 2020. doi:10.1016/j.jaad.2020.01.028 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Haenssle HA, Fink C, Schneiderbauer R, et al. Man against machine: diagnostic performance of a deep learning convolutional neural network for dermoscopic melanoma recognition in comparison to 58 dermatologists. Ann Oncol Off J Eur Soc Med Oncol 2018; 29 (8): 1836–42. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Olsen EA, Canfield D. SALT II: a new take on the Severity of Alopecia Tool (SALT) for determining percentage scalp hair loss. J Am Acad Dermatol 2016; 75 (6): 1268–70. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Gupta AK, Ivanova IA, Renaud HJ. How good is artificial intelligence (AI) at solving hairy problems? A review of AI applications in hair restoration and hair disorders. Dermatol Ther 2021; 34 (2): e14811. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Mukherjee R, Manohar DD, Das DK, Achar A, Mitra A, Chakraborty C. Automated tissue classification framework for reproducible chronic wound assessment. BioMed Res Int 2014; 2014: 851582. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Manohar Dhane D, Maity M, Mungle T, et al. Fuzzy spectral clustering for automated delineation of chronic wound region using digital images. Comput Biol Med 2017; 89: 551–60. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Shrivastava VK, Londhe ND, Sonawane RS, Suri JS. A novel and robust Bayesian approach for segmentation of psoriasis lesions and its risk stratification. Comput Methods Programs Biomed 2017; 150: 9–22. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Adamson AS, Smith A. Machine learning and health care disparities in dermatology. JAMA Dermatol 2018; 154 (11): 1247–8. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Larrazabal AJ, Nieto N, Peterson V, Milone DH, Ferrante E. Gender imbalance in medical imaging datasets produces biased classifiers for computer-aided diagnosis. Proc Natl Acad Sci USA 2020; 117 (23): 12592–4. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Cirillo D, Catuara-Solarz S, Morey C, et al. Sex and gender differences and biases in artificial intelligence for biomedicine and healthcare. NPJ Digit Med 2020; 3: 81. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Olsen CM, Thompson JF, Pandeya N, Whiteman DC. Evaluation of sex-specific incidence of melanoma. JAMA Dermatol 2020; 156 (5): 553–60. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Yuan T-A, Lu Y, Edwards K, Jakowatz J, Meyskens FL, Liu-Smith F. Race-, age-, and anatomic site-specific gender differences in cutaneous melanoma suggest differential mechanisms of early- and late-onset melanoma. IJERPH 2019; 16 (6): 908.doi:10.3390/ijerph16060908 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Ragnarsson-Olding BK, Kanter-Lewensohn LR, Lagerlöf B, Nilsson BR, Ringborg UK. Malignant melanoma of the vulva in a nationwide, 25-year study of 219 Swedish females: clinical observations and histopathologic features. Cancer 1999; 86 (7): 1273–84. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Latini A, Cota C, Orsini D, Cristaudo A, Tedesco M. Male and female genital lichen sclerosus. Clinical and functional classification criteria. Postepy Dermatol Alergol 2018; 35 (5): 447–53. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Sun TY, Walk OJBD IV, Chen JL, Nieva HR, Elhadad N. Exploring gender disparities in time to diagnosis. ArXiv201106100 Cs Stat 2020. http://arxiv.org/abs/2011.06100 Accessed May 8, 2021 [ Google Scholar ] 17. Tan A, Bieber AK, Stein JA, Pomeranz MK. Diagnosis and management of vulvar cancer: a review. J Am Acad Dermatol 2019; 81 (6): 1387–96. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 18. Steen VD, Oddis CV, Conte CG, Janoski J, Casterline GZ, Medsger TA. Incidence of systemic sclerosis in Allegheny County, Pennsylvania. a twenty-year study of hospital-diagnosed cases, 1963–1982. Arthritis Rheum 1997; 40 (3): 441–5. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 19. Petri M. Epidemiology of systemic lupus erythematosus. Best Pract Res Clin Rheumatol 2002; 16 (5): 847–58. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 20. Cartwright T, Endean N, Porter A. Illness perceptions, coping and quality of life in patients with alopecia. Br J Dermatol 2009; 160 (5): 1034–9. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 21. Loussouarn G, Lozano I, Panhard S, Collaudin C, El Rawadi C, Genain G. Diversity in human hair growth, diameter, colour and shape. an in vivo study on young adults from 24 different ethnic groups observed in the five continents. Eur J Dermatol EJD 2016; 26 (2): 144–54. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 22. Birnbaum MR, McLellan BN, Shapiro J, Ye K, Reid SD. Evaluation of hair density in different ethnicities in a healthy American population using quantitative trichoscopic analysis. Skin Appendage Disord 2018; 4 (4): 304–7. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 23. Qian Y, Johannet P, Sawyers A, Yu J, Osman I, Zhong J. The ongoing racial disparities in melanoma: an analysis of the Surveillance, Epidemiology, and End Results database (1975–2016). J Am Acad Dermatol 2021; 84 (6): 1585–93. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 24. Singer S, Tkachenko E, Hartman RI, Mostaghimi A. Association between sexual orientation and lifetime prevalence of skin cancer in the United States. JAMA Dermatol 2020; 156 (4): 441–5. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 25. Yeung H, Braun H, Goodman M. Sexual and gender minority populations and skin cancer-new data and renewed priorities. JAMA Dermatol 2020; 156 (4): 367–9. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 26. Kharazmi P, Kalia S, Lui H, Wang ZJ, Lee TK. A feature fusion system for basal cell carcinoma detection through data-driven feature learning and patient profile. Skin Res Technol 2018; 24 (2): 256–64. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 27. Chen I, Johansson FD, Sontag D. Why is my classifier discriminatory? Adv Neural Inf Process Syst 2018; 31: 3543–54. http://arxiv.org/abs/1805.12002 Accessed May 8, 2021. [ Google Scholar ] 28. Sullivan P, Trinidad J, Hamann D. Issues in transgender dermatology: a systematic review of the literature. J Am Acad Dermatol 2019; 81 (2): 438–47. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 29. Buolamwini J, Gebru T. Gender shades: intersectional accuracy disparities in commercial gender 