INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2678,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10785922','Unsupervised SoftOtsuNet Augmentation for Clinical Dermatology Image Classifiers.','Dominguez M, Finnell JT.','2024','dnns furniture dnns foreground prohibitively differentiable otsu cutout softotsunet fitzpatrick17k softotsunet commodity dnns dnns dnns informatician distributional cropping brightness rectangle cutout randomness memorize otsu thresholding eigenvector laplacian otsu greyscale otsu bimodal binarization smarter cutout otsu differentiable cutout settle minima softotsunet efficientnet preprocessed softotsunet preprocessed propagates forwards backwards softotsunet convolutions baselines otsu otsu notation notation notation elementwise otsu otsu greyscale luminance otsu exploits greyscale bimodal otsu greyscale greyscale otsu exhaustively otsu greyscale onerous otsu otsu greyscale luminance unet rgb luminance pixelwise luminance differentiable otsu greyscale memberships otsu otsu otsu pushes hw1 pushes otsu greyscale otsu foreground greyscale otsu greyscale rgb greyscale luminance itu superscripts exponents greyscale brightness brightness luminance softotsu luminance softmax otsu luminance softotsu cutout luminance coefficents otsu luminance learnable cleaner smoothness smoothness smoothness laplacian classcut grabcut weinshall stride interpolate bilinear interpolation aliasing pixelwise detaching backpropagation softotsunet rgb foreground unprocessed cutout cutout softotsunet greyscale settles minima cutout rectangle rectangle cutout softotsu softotsunet settling softotsu detach backpropagation softotsunet softotsunet otsu softotsunet softotsunet unmask unmasked 17k splits efficientnetb4 flips randaugment randaugment cutout cutout randaugment softotsunet randaugment nesterov cosine restarts pytorch autocast computations initialized efficientnet imagenet softot sunet initialized kaiming initialization wdot softotsunet splits fitzpatrick17k splits splits splits splits visualdx fitzpatrick17k fitzpatrick17k cutout randaugment fitzpatrick17k splits fitzpatrick17k splits inequal randaugment cutout randaugment fitzpatrick17k svms splits fitzpatrick17k splits cutout randaug cutout softotsunet randaugment visualdx visualdx convnext imagenet 22k imagenet convnext adamw daugment nfnet randaugment cutout identically softotsunet visualdx commodity pixelwise astronomical informaticians luminance greyscale otsu thresholding blur greyscale zech badgeley titano oermann e1002683 sagawa marklund meila wilds 38th pmlr v139 koh21a devries cutout cisse dauphin mixup cutmix localizable cvf iccv ronneberger brox navab hornegger frangi miccai otsu cybernetics chaudhuri salakhutdinov efficientnet 36th pmlr hdtv scholkopf laplacian alexe deselaers classcut daniilidis maragos paragios eccv rother grabcut foreground iterated weinshall foreground groh soenksen 17k cvpr cubuk zoph shlens randaugment larochelle ranzato hadsell balcan sutskever initialization mcallester pmlr loshchilov hutter sgdr restarts szegedy vanhoucke ioffe shlens wojna cvpr socher imagenet luong hovy imagenet cvf cvpr delving rectifiers imagenet iccv daneshjou barata stablein celebi codella combalia rivista economica daneshjou vodrahalli novoa eabq6147 sciadv abq6147 feichtenhofer darrell convnet 2020s cvf cvpr loshchilov hutter decoupled krizhevsky sutskever salakhutdinov simonyan meila 38th pmlr','Abstract Data Augmentation is a crucial tool in the Machine Learning (ML) toolbox because it can extract novel, useful training images from an existing dataset, thereby improving accuracy and reducing overfitting in a Deep Neural Network (DNNs). However, clinical dermatology images often contain irrelevant background information,such as furniture and objects in the frame. DNNs make use of that information when optimizing the loss function. Data augmentation methods that preserve this information risk creating biases in the DNN’s understanding (for example, that objects in a particular doctor’s office are a clue that the patient has cutaneous T-cell lymphoma). Creating a supervised foreground/background segmentation algorithm for clinical dermatology images that removes this irrelevant information would be prohibitively expensive due to labeling costs. To that end, we propose a novel unsupervised DNN that dynamically masks out image information based on a combination of a differentiable adaptation of Otsu’s Method and CutOut augmentation. SoftOtsuNet augmentation outperforms all other evaluated augmentation methods on the Fitzpatrick17k dataset ( 0.75% improvement), Diverse Dermatology Images dataset ( 1.76% improvement), and our proprietary dataset ( 0.92% improvement). SoftOtsuNet is only required at training time, meaning inference costs are unchanged from the baseline. This further suggests that even large data-driven models can still benefit from human-engineered unsupervised loss functions. Introduction Many medical imaging modalities (radiography, ultrasound, dermoscopy, CT Scans, and FMRI) are designed to only capture information about the subject, and not the environment the subject is in. This reduces one potential avenue for bias when creating a Machine Learning (ML) algorithm from these images. This is not the case with clinical dermatology images captured with commodity cameras or smartphones. While some of these images may be composed such that the lesion and its surrounding skin take up the entire image, others are taken in front of a backdrop (such as a blue screen) or even in the context of a clinician’s office. If the images are taken by consumers themselves, the objects in their homes may be present. These irrelevant background details are a source of bias for ML algorithms that attempt to predict diagnoses or lesion morphologies. Deep Neural Networks (DNNs), especially Convolutional Neural Networks, have become the dominant tool for computer vision ML applications. Given a large training dataset of pairs (X,y) where X is an image and y is a label (e.g. a categorical or “class” label), DNNs can learn a function that maps the training images to the training labels. If the dataset is large enough, DNNs often generalize well to unseen images. However, by default an informatician has no direct control over what parts of the image the DNN uses to decide its prediction. In a Psoriasis prediction task, the DNN will learn from the Psoriasis lesions in the image, but not exclusively. If the collected training set is sourced heavily from a single region, demographic, or clinician, then markers of these sources will factor into the prediction. If this model is deployed in the field, then the accuracy users experience will be markedly lower than that reported on the internal test set. These subtle distributional shifts are a key concern when deploying AI in live healthcare settings. 1 , 2 Traditionally, these biases are alleviated by either collecting larger datasets or through data augmentation algorithms. Larger datasets are expensive to collect and label. Images of certain diseases may be difficult to collect due to their rarity. Data augmentation is a process by which training images undergo superficial image processing operations to produce a “new” sample that would still appear different to the neural network. Examples include random cropping, brightness/contrast/color adjustments, replacing a random rectangle in the image with noise (CutOut), 3 or mixing two images together. 4 , 5 These transformations introduce variety into the training data that helps the DNN generalize better to unseen images, but none of them directly address the concern of irrelevant background details in clinical dermatology images. They simply inject randomness into the training. If one had an algorithm that could mask out portions of the background pixels with random noise when training, then the classifier would memorize fewer erroneous patterns. Indeed, this type of algorithm is feasible with supervised segmentation networks such as U-Net, 6 but we again run into the challenge of costly collection and labeling of data. Segmentation networks trained in a supervised fashion need per-pixel labels that are much more costly to collect than per-image labels. We seek inspiration in pre-Deep Learning segmentation algorithms, such as Normalized Graph Cuts 7 or Otsu’s Method. 8 These methods are useful because they exploit prior assumptions about images that are often true. Normalized Graph Cuts demonstrate that a useful binary segmentation can often be found by thresholding the second-smallest eigenvector of a Graph Laplacian formed from an image. Otsu’s Method demonstrates a useful segmentation from the histogram of pixel values in a greyscale representation of an image. Otsu observed that many such histograms are bimodal, and a useful binarization of an image can be formed by placing a threshold in the valley between the two modes of the histogram, setting all the pixel values below the threshold to 0 and all the pixel values above to 1. We argue that these priors could be used to create a smarter “CutOut” algorithm that specifically seeks out regions in the background of the image. The discrete cost function that Otsu minimizes can be adapted into a differentiable one that can be optimized with gradient descent while having the same effect. It can also be modified to take into account location, encouraging CutOut-like behavior of masking out contiguous regions. In doing so we create a context-aware masking network that is completely unsupervised. We engineer the training of this network so that it does not settle on a single local minima per training example, promoting continued augmentation and learning throughout the entire training process. Best of all, this network, which we call SoftOtsuNet, is only required during training and can be discarded at inference time. Methods Our general neural network architecture is presented in Figure 1 . We make use of an off-the-shelf U-Net 6 segmentation network and an off-the-shelf classifier network, such as EfficientNet, 9 in series. The U-Net shall produce a mask that will “replace” mix the input image with uniform random noise such that background pixels are mostly noise. This mixed image is then passed into a classifier network. The classifier only learns from standard cross entropy classification loss, while the U-Net learns only from an unsupervised prior loss that clusters the pixels into two groups. The pair of networks is trained simultaneously, but at inference time only the classifier network is used. Figure 1: Open in a new tab Architecture of our work. Training images are preprocessed by our “SoftOtsuNet” to replace a portion of the image with random noise based on unsupervised priors. This preprocessed image is then passed into a classifier. The dotted line indicates that between the two networks, data only propagates forwards and not backwards (no gradient signal from the classifier passes into SoftOtsuNet). We do not tune the architectures of these networks (number of layers, number of convolutions per layer, amount of DropOut, etc) as part of our effort to surpass the baselines. All of the development in this work is in engineering the U-Net to produce useful masks that lend themselves to useful augmentation in the classifier. This consists of two tasks: adapting our hypothesized useful prior, Otsu’s Method, to a neural network context, and overcoming Otsu’s limitations that may hamper its utility. For clarity we include Table 1 as a notation guide for commonly used symbols in the equations in this work. Table 1: Frequently used notation in this work Notation Meaning µ X Sample mean of array X σ X Sample variance of array X h, w Pixel location indices for an image of size H × W x hw An individual location in an image array X ⊙ Elementwise multiplication Open in a new tab A Review of Otsu’s Method Otsu’s method attempts to threshold a grayscale image into consists of two components: A loss function that defines the quality of a binary segmentation (1) and a single discrete parameter by which to optimize it. The parameters p 0 and p 1 are the fraction of pixels that are in class 0 and class 1 respectively. The parameters σ 0 2 and σ 1 2 are the sample variances of the greyscale Luminance pixels in class 0 and class 1 respectively. A minimum L otsu cost should minimize the intra-class variance of the pixels in each class and maximize their inter-class variance. This algorithm exploits the observation that greyscale images often (but not always) have bimodal histograms. L o t s u = p 0 σ 0 2 + p 1 σ 1 2 (1) The parameter by which L otsu is optimized is the threshold in a greyscale histogram such that every greyscale pixel value to the left of the threshold is assigned to class 0 and every other pixel value is assigned to class 1. Otsu is a discrete optimization algorithm that exhaustively searches through all possible thresholds to minimize L otsu . Given that there is only one parameter that in an 8-bit greyscale image only takes 256 values, this exhaustive search is not onerous. Defining a Soft Otsu We intend to use our U-Net to produce an Otsu-like mask. We define a single per-pixel output function (2) that represents the probability m hw 1 a given greyscale “Luminance” pixel value x L hw is in class 1, given the current parameters of a U-Net Θ UNet and the input RGB image X ∈ R H × W × 3 (we discuss converting to the Luminance image X L ∈ ℝ H × W in the next section). The values are bound to be valid probabilities through the use of a sigmoid function σ(x) . The corresponding probability that the pixel is in class 0 is one minus the probability that it is in class one. If we take the average of all the pixelwise predictions in the Luminance image (3), we get the differentiable values p soft 0 and p soft 1 correspond to the hard fractions of pixel membership p 0 and p 1 in the original L otsu . The individual elements of m hw 1 in array form M ∈ R H × W is referred to as the “mask”. m h w 1 = P ( x L h w = 1 | Θ U N e t ) = σ ( f ( X , Θ U N e t ) ) m h w 0 = P ( x L h w = 0 | Θ U N e t ) = 1 − P ( x L h w = 1 | Θ U N e t ) (2) P s o f t c = ∑ h , w 1 H W P ( x L h w = c | Θ U N e t ) , c ∈ { 0 , 1 } (3) These probability are real numbers between 0 and 1, not exactly 0 and 1. As a result, every pixel has a partial membership in class 0 and class 1. This means that the variance computation for the greyscale representation of the image X g will have to take into account these partial memberships. We can produce two “soft membership images” (4) from the mask M , from which we compute the intra-class variances. The sample variances σ X L o 2 and S i g m a X L 1 2 of these soft membership images represent our soft analogues to σ 0 2 and σ 1 2 in L otsu . X L 1 = M ⊙ X L , X L 0 = ( 1 − M ) ⊙ X L (4) We could now attempt to simply replace all of the terms in L otsu with our soft equivalents to produce a soft Otsu loss (5), but this would be insufficient to give us the effect we desire. Now that our terms are continuous and not discrete, there is nothing in them that pushes the U-Net predictions to be close to 0 or 1. It is plausible that individual mask values m hw1 of around 0.5 across the board could minimize the intra-class variance, but this would not maximize inter-class variance (a behavior that occurs naturally in the original discrete formulation). We must add an additional term that pushes the sample means of the two soft membership images to be as far apart as possible. We accomplish this with a negative exponential distance metric (6). Note that the only mechanism by which to push the means apart (until the next section) is for the network to push the individual mask values higher or lower. L int r a var = p s o f t 0 σ X L 1 2 + p s o f t 1 σ X L 1 2 (5) L m e a n s e p = e − ( μ X L 0 − μ X L 1 ) 2 (6) With these two loss functions combined, we now have a plausible adaptation of Otsu’s loss function to a segmentation neural network context. Note that we no longer require that all greyscale pixels of a given value be assigned to the same class, and we have many more free parameters in the U-Net than in Otsu’s original algorithm. Nonetheless we should get comparable behavior. There are two implementation details that we have not yet discussed. The first is how to account for the fact that class 0 is not always the background and class 1 is not always the background. What we do is compare the sample means µ M and µ (1−M) . If µ M is greater for a given image, we pass M forward as the mask, otherwise we pass (1 − M ) forward as the mask. We assume that the cluster with fewer masked-out pixels is the foreground. We now turn our attention to the other missing detail: how to attain the greyscale image X g from X , from which we compute our Otsu cost function. Converting to Greyscale Converting an image from RGB to a single-channel greyscale Luminance image can be a trivial operation. There are simple linear formulas based on human perception, such as the formula (7) proposed in ITU-R Recommendation BT.709. 10 In this formula the r , g , and b superscripts do not denote exponents, but instead denote the red, green and blue channels, respectively, of the color image. x L h w = 0.2125 x h w r + 0.7154 x h w g + 0.0721 x h w b (7) The coefficients sum to 1 and are chosen to produce a greyscale image with the same perceived brightness as the color image. These constraints (a single global formula that maintains brightness) are not critical to our current application, however. Our actual goal is to produce a single-channel image whose histogram has two low-variance, clearly separated modes. We could better achieve this with a more general formulation of (7) based on local and global features of every individual image. For example, our U-Net could output per-pixel, per-channel Luminance coefficients as a function of the input image (8). This gives L softotsu another variable to minimize by allowing the Luminance conversion formula to be learned per pixel per image. We can keep the constraint that the per-channel coefficients for a given pixel prediction sum to 1 by applying a softmax function to the vector of predicted coefficients for each pixel. This keeps the interpretation of each coefficient as the relative importance of each channel compared to the others. x L h w = ∑ c h a n n e l ∈ C f c h a n n e l h w ( x , Θ U n e t ) x h w c h a n n e l (8) We go one step further and expand the set of channels C. Normally in Otsu’s method, C = { r , g , b } , but we expand it to include normalized (x,y) coordinates of each pixel (ranging from -1 to 1): C = { r , g , b , x , y } . This would produce a Luminance channel such that L softotsu would attempt to cluster the pixels by both color and location simultaneously, thus localizing the mask. We find that this is more effective than localizing only by color. Instead of attempting to segment the image by color, we attempt to produce a dynamically shaped Cutout-style patch during each training iteration. Whether we use fixed Luminance coefficients, dynamic per-pixel coefficients based on color, or dynamic per-pixel coefficents based on both color and location, we maintain the same motivation as Otsu. We seek to generate a mask by separating the pixels in the image into two clearly separate distributions, maximizing inter-class variance and minimizing intra-class variance. By making the Luminance function more dynamic and learnable, we achieve a cleaner separation. Smoothness Prior We assert that the boundary between the two pixel clusters of an image should be smooth . If the subject is a body location with a lesion, prominent in the frame, then it is reasonable that the output mask should contain low frequency details. To encourage this, we add an additional smoothness loss to the Masking Network. One such model for smoothness is Laplacian smoothing, 11 where similar pixels (by location, color, or both) are encouraged to have a similar classification. Different smoothing loss functions are used in pre-Deep Learning unsupervised segmentation works such as ClassCut, 12 GrabCut, 13 and Rosenfeld and Weinshall. 14 We propose a smoothing loss that encourages the network to shed high frequency detail in the mask M . Within the network we average pool the currently predicted mask with a 2 × 2 window and a stride of 2. Next, we interpolate the pooled mask back to original size with bilinear interpolation and an anti-aliasing filter. This produces a weak “ground truth” mask M LP , representing a “low-pass” filtered Mask that removes high frequency detail. We then compute the pixelwise binary cross entropy (9) between M and M LP . This is an appropriate loss function in our case because we can safely assume the values range between 0 and 1 and can thus be interpreted as probabilities. The stop function represents a detaching of the weak ground truth M LP from the backpropagation graph. This means that the predictions are adjusted to match M LP and not the other way around. L s m o o t h = ∑ h , w s t o p ( m L P h w ) log ( m h w ) + ( 1 − s t o p ( m L P h w ) ) log ( 1 − m h w ) (9) We sum our intra-class variance loss, mean separation loss, and smoothing loss to produce the total unsupervised loss (10) for the SoftOtsuNet. L s o f t o t s u = L int r a var + L m e a n s e p + L s m o o t h (10) Putting it all Together We now have all of the individual components for our augmentation algorithm defined. Now we need to integrate them into a single training pipeline. First we must produce an augmented image from our U-Net network. We combine the RGB image X ∈ ℝ H × W × 3 with an array of uniform random noise X ∈ ℝ H × W × 3 using our predicted mask M (11). An example of this preprocessing is in Figure 3 . The resulting image does have the desired effect: The background is much attenuated, while the foreground is preserved. That said, this masked image is of a different distribution than the unprocessed images. As the authors of CutOut 3 discovered, an augmentation that masks part of the image can result in a network that is flexible about the parts of the image it learns from, but it can also come to expect masks of random noise at inference time. The lack of such masking at inference time can lower the accuracy. CutOut solved this issue by only applying the masks to a random 50% of the samples during training, and we follow their lead. Thus we get both the benefits of masking and of natural backgrounds. Figure 3: Open in a new tab From left to right: An original color image, its predicted SoftOtsuNet mask, its learned greyscale mapping, and the augmented image that would be passed to the classifier. X a u g = M ʘ X + ( 1 − M ) ʘ N (11) One observation we have found when training with this loss is that the Masking Network settles into a local minima fairly quickly, reducing the amount of variability (and thus augmentation) that comes from different mask solutions. To resolve this, we incorporate CutOut into the masking output. The predicted mask M has a random additional rectangle set to zero. This has the effect of setting that rectangle of pixels to be random noise (as in traditional CutOut), but also adding noise to the statistics used to compute L softotsu , preventing SoftOtsuNet from settling into predicting the same static mask for every training image at every iteration. Finally, we need to combine our novel L softotsu loss with traditional cross entropy classification loss L ce (12). Note that while this combined loss is used to optimize the two networks together, the individual costs are kept separate. We “detach” the gradient of the backpropagation graph in-between SoftOtsuNet and the classifier, which results in the cross entropy loss having no effect on SoftOtsuNet, and the Soft Otsu priors having no effect on the classifier. The reason that we do this, rather than have the classifier send a gradient signal to SoftOtsuNet, is that we observe that the classifier tends to encourage the SoftOtsuNet to unmask as many pixels as possible. This makes intuitive sense, more unmasked pixels means that it is easier to fit the training set. However, it comes at the cost of validation set performance. L = L c e + L s o f t o t s u (12) Training Setup We first evaluate our algorithm on the Fitzpatrick 17k dataset, 15 a publicly available large-scale labeled Clinical Dermatology dataset which consists of 16,577 images across 114 diagnoses, representing all six Fitzpatrick skin types. We cross validate with five random stratified splits of 80% training and 20% validation (separate from the split on which we tuned our algorithm). We compare the following methods: BL : An EfficientNetB4 9 classifier trained with only minimal augmentation (random flips and crops). RA : The Baseline , but additionally trained with RandAugment ( M = 15 , N = 1) 16 augmentation. These RandAugment parameters were chosen from the original paper based on the best empirical results for a dataset of roughly 10, 000 training samples. CO : The Baseline , but additionally trained with CutOut 3 augmentation. CO+RA : The Baseline , but additionally trained with both CutOut and RandAugment augmentation. Ours : The Baseline trained with our novel SoftOtsuNet augmentation network. Ours + RA : Ours trained additionally with RandAugment augmentation. All networks are trained for 250 epochs with a batch size of 60, stochastic gradient descent with an initial learning rate of 0.01, a Nesterov momentum 17 parameter of 0.9, and a cosine decay learning schedule (without warm restarts). 18 We apply label smoothing (α = 0.2) to the one-hot classification labels. 19 We train using PyTorch’s autocast function so that the computations are performed with 16-bit floating point values. The classifier networks are initialized with pretrained weights from an EfficientNet trained on ImageNet data 20 and Noisy Student self-training. 21 The SoftOt-suNet is simply randomly initialized with Kaiming initialization. 22 We open source the code sufficient to reproduce this evaluation at https://github.com/WDot/SoftOtsuNet . Results Our results on five separate 80% training/20% validation splits of the Fitzpatrick17k dataset are in Table 2 . Our model matches or outperforms every other method on every split, yielding the highest average accuracy over five splits and an improvement of 0.75% over the baseline. Evaluating each method on multiple splits reduces the chance that a given result is due to luck. Each of the baseline splits produces a slightly different accuracy. Even among the competing data augmentation methods, there are some splits that yield an improvement and some that do not. As a result, the competing methods result in a slightly lower average accuracy than the baseline! This is not to say that these data augmentation methods are not useful, as has been demonstrated on other computer vision benchmarks. 3,16 Indeed, the VisualDx networks made use of both methods until this work, finding them to yield consistent improvements on a much larger dataset than Fitzpatrick17k. However, given the size of Fitzpatrick17k and the variety of classes being modeled, it is easy for biases from irrelevant background details to persist in the random variability introduced in both Cutout and RandAugment. Table 2: Five-fold Accuracies on the Fitzpatrick17k Dataset Split BL CO RA CO+RA Ours Ours + RA 1 66.6% 66.9% 66.9% 66.9% 67.5% 67.7% 2 65.2% 65.1% 65.3% 65.1% 65.6% 65.6% 3 66.5% 66.0% 66.5% 66.0% 67.0% 67.0% 4 67.5% 67.7% 67.5% 67.7% 68.0% 67.9% 5 66.0% 65.6% 65.2% 65.6% 66.2% 66.2% Mean 66.4% 66.3% 66.3% 66.3% 66.9% 66.9% Open in a new tab We can drill down and assess the accuracies per Fitzpatrick skin type (averaged over all five splits) in Table 3 . By characterizing these methods in terms of per-demographic accuracy, we are adhering to CLEAR Derm AI standards (especially point 21), which recommends that per-demographic results be published in addition to overall results. 23 As a summary measure of inequity, we also report the Gini score 24 (13) over all the per-demographic accuracies a i . Gini is often employed as a measure of income inequality. In this work we use it as a metric for assessing equity among accuracies. A Gini score of 0 means perfect equity among demographic accuracies, whereas a Gini score approaching 1 means highly inequitable algorithm performance. Table 3: Mean Five-fold per-Fitzpatrick Skin Type Accuracies on the Fitzpatrick17k Dataset Overall Acc I II III IV V VI Gini BL 66.4% 56.6% 60.6% 69.6% 76.0% 77.7% 69.7% 0.060 RA 66.3% 55.9% 60.3% 70.1% 76.0% 78.6% 69.4% 0.070 CO 66.3% 56.6% 59.8% 70.1% 76.3% 77.0% 70.9% 0.063 CO+RA 66.3% 56.6% 59.8% 70.5% 76.2% 77.2% 70.9% 0.063 Ours 66.9% 57.1% 61.1% 70.6% 76.5% 76.8% 70.6% 0.060 Ours + RA 66.9% 56.5% 61.2% 71.1% 76.5% 78.1% 70.0% 0.064 Open in a new tab ∑ i = 0 F − 1 ∑ j = 0 F − 1 | a i − a j | 2 F Σ i = 0 F − 1 (13) Our data augmentation method yields the top accuracy for three out of the six Fitzpatrick skin types in the dataset on average over the five splits. It maintains the same Gini score as the Baseline, while the other methods slightly increase the overall Gini score (and thus have slightly more inequal accuracies). Granted, the Gini scores are all similar–none of the methods leads to a catastrophic failure to predict accurately for a particular skin type. However, RandAugment and Cutout + RandAugment yield the top skin type V and VI accuracies, respectively, at a small expense to other skin types. Note that none of the methods under evaluation explicitly condition on Fitzpatrick skin type, so the Gini metric is not “engineered”. However, we can observe that different methods of adding random variability to the training data can increase or decrease bias independently of overall performance. Diverse Dermatology Images Dataset We next evaluate our trained networks on the Diverse Dermatology Images (DDI) Dataset. 25 This is a small dataset of 656 clinical dermatology images representing 78 separate diagnoses categorized into either “malignant” or “not malignant”. This is too small a dataset, with too many unique classes, to effectively train a standard supervised Deep Neural Network. Instead, we extract features for each of the images from each of the Fitzpatrick17k-trained networks in Table 2 and use them to train linear Support Vector Machine (SVM) classifiers. This exercise shall evaluate how well each of the trained networks are able to linearly separate malignant/not malignant in an unseen dataset. We train each of the SVMs in five-fold cross validation, with five splits of 80% training and 20% validation. We also evaluate the features of each of the networks trained in the five separate Fitzpatrick17k splits, resulting in 25 total evaluations of DDI per method. The results are in Table 4 . Note that in DDI, the Fitzpatrick skin types are grouped into three clusters: I/II, III/IV, and V/VI. In this case several methods (including ours) beat the baseline by an equal amount (1.76%). However the difference is in the average Gini score over the 25 runs. Our methods achieve a lower Gini score on average than the other methods. Cutout and Randaug + CutOut achieve their accuracy by being more accurate on skin types V/VI at the expense of skin types I/II, while our method has similar performance for both groups. Given that “Ours” and “Ours + RA” have similar mean accuracies, but considerably different mean Gini scores, we also list the standard deviations in Table 4 to show that combining SoftOtsuNet with RandAugment decreases the standard deviation on Fitzpatrick skin types I/II and V/VI, leading to an overall more equitable algorithm. Table 4: Mean 5x Five-fold Accuracies on the Diverse Dermatology Images Dataset Overall Acc I/II Acc III/IV Acc V/VI Acc Gini BL 73.8% ± 4.56% 74.3% ± 8.65% 73.2% ± 5.58% 74.4% ± 5.40% 0.032 ± 0.032 RA 74.9% ± 2.97% 75.0% ± 4.87% 72.3% ± 5.97% 78.2% ± 6.03% 0.033 ± 0.017 CO 75.1% ± 3.82% 72.8% ± 5.72% 73.9% ± 5.43% 79.0% ± 6.30% 0.033 ± 0.014 CO+RA 75.1% ± 3.82% 72.8% ± 5.72% 73.9% ± 5.43% 79.0% ± 6.30% 0.033 ± 0.014 Ours 75.1% ± 3.31% 76.4% ± 7.14% 72.7% ± 4.20% 76.9% ± 6.03% 0.031 ± 0.016 Ours + RA 74.9% ± 3.50% 76.5% ± 4.72% 72.7% ± 5.35% 76.4% ± 5.42% 0.025 ± 0.012 Open in a new tab VisualDx Internal Dataset Finally, we evaluate our method on the proprietary VisualDx dataset. This dataset consists of 130,663 images with three associated labeled tasks. “Expert” diagnosis prediction (E) has the model predict a diagnosis from 269 separate classes. “Consumer” diagnosis prediction (C) is a strict subset of “Expert” diagnosis prediction, with 191 separate classes. Finally, “Lesion” prediction (L) has the model predict lesion morphology from an image, with 80 separate classes. Of the 130,663 images, 86,157 have diagnosis labels and 107,841 have lesion labels (many have both). We evaluate our networks on a single fresh split of 80% training, 10% validation, and 10% test. We first tune the baseline network on the validation set, finding that a ConvNext-Base backbone 26 pretrained on ImageNet 22k and ImageNet 1k fits the data best. We use ConvNext’s recommended fine-tuning optimization settings of AdamW 27 optimization with an initial learning rate of 5 e − 5. This network is able to learn the data quickly, so we apply aggressive Dropout ( p = 0.5) 28 before the linear layer that predicts each task and augment the data with more aggressive Ran-dAugment ( M = 10, N = 4, taking a cue from NFNet 29 which also aggressively applies RandAugment) as well as CutOut. We train the network for 50 epochs. Otherwise our training settings are the same as the Fitzpatrick experiments. We compare the baseline against the identically trained network, with our SoftOtsuNet. The results on the test set are in Table 5 . We can see that the Expert 269 class task is significantly improved, while the other tasks match or surpass the performance of the baseline. Table 5: Accuracies on the VisualDx Dataset E Top-1 E Top-3 C Top-1 C Top-3 L Top-1 L Top-3 Baseline 54.6% 73.7% 61.2% 79.9% 52.8% 80.5% Our Method 55.1% 74.2% 61.2% 80.1% 52.8% 80.5% Open in a new tab Conclusion Large datasets and Deep Neural Networks have proven themselves superior to classical “feature engineering” in computer vision applications. However, this does not mean that automatic diagnosis from an image has been reduced to the task of gathering and labeling data. Especially in the field of dermatology, the most economical method of gathering images is from commodity cameras used by either clinicians or patients themselves, and this means that irrelevant background information is almost certainly part of the data. Finding and exploiting useful statistical priors about clinical data can open up means of cleaning and augmenting the data in concert with an unsupervised neural network. This not only improves the classifier but it does so in a more scalable way than manual pixelwise labeling of background data or hand-editing of images, both of which would be impose astronomical costs on informaticians. Feature Engineering may have given way to Deep Learning, but mere supervised Deep Learning shall give way to Prior Engineering, combining the advantages of both big data and deep expert domain knowledge for superior results. Figures & Tables Figure 2: Open in a new tab From left to right: An original color image, its Luminance (greyscale) representation, Otsu thresholding (after Gaussian blur, σ = 5 ), and greyscale Histogram. References 1. Zech JR, Badgeley MA, Liu M, Costa AB, Titano JJ, Oermann EK. PLoS medicine. 11. Vol. 15. Place: United States: 2018 Nov. Variable generalization performance of a deep learning model to detect pneumonia in chest radiographs: A cross-sectional study; p. e1002683. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Koh PW, Sagawa S, Marklund H, et al. Meila M, Zhang T, editors. WILDS: A