yst Rev. 2016;(8). [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials 12913_2021_6954_MOESM1_ESM.docx (14KB, docx) Additional file 1. Post-intervention survey. 12913_2021_6954_MOESM2_ESM.docx (14KB, docx) Additional file 2. Pre-intervention survey. Data Availability Statement The datasets used and/or analyzed during the current study are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request.',689,'2025-10-14 06:53:43','2025-10-14 06:53:43');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2682,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9996452','Patient Perspectives on Clinic Note Transparency Within Dermatology.','Yang K, Lau CB, Lau WC, Nambudiri VE, Watson AJ.','2023','mychart jasp cancel hebrew cantonese burmese uninterpretable jargon desroches leveille e201753 leveille opennotes e13876 hibbard nahmias opennotes ijwd yanovsky mostaghimi buzney blease torous hagglund','Abstract This survey study assesses dermatology patient experiences with viewing online medical records and seeks to identify areas for improvement. Under the 21st Century Cures Act, US health care systems are mandated to give patients electronic access to information in their medical records to promote health care transparency. 1 , 2 Research evaluating patient experiences with accessing their dermatology clinic notes is currently limited. In this survey study, we aimed to assess dermatology patient experiences with viewing online medical records and sought to identify areas for improvement. Methods An anonymous, voluntary survey (eAppendix in Supplement 1 ) was offered to a convenience sample of all patients presenting at 2 Brigham and Women’s Hospital–affiliated dermatology clinics on selected days over a 2-month period from February to April 2022 as part of a quality-improvement initiative. As a quality-improvement project, this study was deemed exempt from review by the institutional review board at Brigham and Women’s Hospital. Patients completed surveys in clinic waiting rooms and were asked about demographic information, utilization of the hospital system’s electronic portal Patient Gateway (PG; an instance of MyChart [Epic]), and opinions regarding dermatology clinic note access. Analysis was performed using JASP statistical software, version 0.16 (University of Amsterdam). Results Among 359 survey respondents, 343 (95.5%) had enrolled in PG ( Table 1 ). Respondents most frequently used PG to view laboratory results (n = 319 [88.9%]) and clinical notes (n = 302 [84.1%]). A total of 280 patients (78.0%) reported having previously read dermatology clinic notes on PG, and 254 (90.4%) of those patients felt positively or very positively about being able to read their dermatology clinic notes ( Table 2 ). Notably, no respondents reported negative sentiment toward having access to dermatology notes. Most patients felt that having access to dermatology clinic notes helped them understand their medical condition better (n = 196 [70.0%]) and fostered increased involvement in their care (n = 180 [64.3%]). A minority reported such access also enhanced their ability to discuss dermatologic diagnoses with other health care professionals (n = 117 [41.8%]) and improved skin self-care (n = 83 [29.6%]). Table 1. Demographics of Survey Respondents. Characteristic No. (%) No. of respondents 359 Age, y <25 45 (12.5) 25-40 100 (27.9) 41-55 65 (18.1) 56-70 103 (28.7) >70 46 (12.8) Gender Female 243 (67.7) Male 111 (30.9) Nonbinary 2 (0.6) Prefer not to say 3 (0.8) Race and ethnicity American Indian 3 (0.8) Asian or Pacific Islander 22 (6.1) Black or African American 39 (10.9) Hispanic 33 (9.2) White 261 (72.7) Other a 1 (0.3) Primary language English 343 (95.5) Spanish 9 (2.5) Other b 7 (2.0) Education level Some high school 4 (1.1) High school diploma 56 (15.6) College degree 157 (43.7) Graduate degree 142 (39.6) Signed up for Patient Gateway Yes 343 (95.5) Uncertain 1 (0.3) No 15 (4.2) How patient uses Patient Gateway View laboratory results 319 (88.9) View notes 302 (84.1) View biopsy/pathology results 232 (64.6) Ask for refills 194 (54.0) Change/modify/cancel appointments 226 (63.0) Send dermatologist questions 255 (71.0) Ever read dermatology clinic notes on Patient Gateway Yes 280 (78.0) No 79 (22.0) Open in a new tab a This respondent did not clarify their ethnicity. b A small number of additional primary languages were reported, including Hebrew, Russian, Italian, Cantonese, Portuguese, and Burmese. Table 2. Patient Experiences With Dermatology Clinic Notes (N = 280). Patient experience No. (%) How patient feels about being able to read dermatology clinic notes Very positive 152 (54.3) Positive 101 (36.1) Neutral 27 (9.6) Negative 0 Very negative 0 Patient-perceived benefits regarding notes Reading dermatology notes helped improve understanding of medical condition 196 (70.0) Reading dermatology notes helped improve understanding of treatment plan 169 (60.4) Takes better care of skin as a result of having access to dermatology clinic notes 83 (29.6) Feels more involved in care as a result of having access to dermatology clinic notes 180 (64.3) Trusts dermatologist more after reading dermatology clinic notes 99 (35.4) Better able to discuss skin condition with other health care professionals 117 (41.8) Patient-perceived concerns regarding notes There was missing information in dermatology clinic notes 18 (6.4) There was inaccurate information in dermatology clinic notes 12 (4.3) Instructions in dermatology clinic notes were difficult to follow 7 (2.5) Did not understand what some of the words in dermatology clinic notes meant 29 (10.4) Did not understand abbreviations in dermatology clinic notes 27 (9.6) Trust dermatologist less after reading dermatology clinic notes 5 (1.8) Ever contact dermatologist with questions about dermatology clinic notes 18 (6.4) Open in a new tab Patient concerns regarding clinic notes were limited, including not understanding certain terminologies (n = 29 [10.4%]) and uninterpretable abbreviations (n = 27 [9.6%]). Five patients (1.8%) reported trusting their dermatologist less after reading their dermatology notes. Another 18 respondents (6.4%) reported contacting their dermatologist with questions after reading their notes. In open-ended responses, patients acknowledged the convenience of accessing dermatology notes, appreciated the notes as references for clinic visits, and preferred more detail to less detail. Discussion Among respondents in this survey study, most viewed having access to dermatology clinic notes favorably. More than half reported that reading their dermatology notes resulted in improved understanding of their medical condition and treatment plan, as well as increased feelings of involvement in their care. These findings are important because engaged patients are more likely to adhere to treatment plans, resulting in better health outcomes. 3 Additionally, they shed light on patient perspectives toward medical record transparency following implementation of the 21st Century Cures Act, further enhancing the context provided by prior studies examining a home-grown medical record system. 4 A minority of patients expressed concerns over missing/inaccurate information, unclear instructions, and ambiguous terminology, suggesting areas for potential enhancement. To optimize patient experiences, clear and unambiguous language should be used, without compromising clinical documentation. A previously published study noted that while patients may enjoy note accessibility, misinterpretation of benign diagnoses may cause anxiety. 5 Another study suggested that implementation of supportive, empathic, and encouraging wording into documentation can strengthen patient trust. 6 Dermatology notes may be differentially accessed compared with other specialties, given typically quick clinical encounters, numerous diagnoses per visit, distinctive diagnostic terminology or jargon, and tendency for visits to include evaluation, management, and procedures happening concomitantly; such factors could drive more frequent dermatology documentation review. This study’s limitations include its single-institutional nature, highly educated urban demographic, and primarily White population, which may affect its generalizability. Additionally, given the survey distribution methods, we were unable to accurately assess exact response rates. Further research should explore dermatologist perspectives on patient note transparency and understanding the longitudinal relationship between access to notes and patient health outcomes. Supplement 1. eAppendix. Survey Click here for additional data file. (237.4KB, pdf) Supplement 2. Data Sharing Statement Click here for additional data file. (13.1KB, pdf) References 1. DesRoches CM, Leveille S, Bell SK, et al. The views and experiences of clinicians sharing medical record notes with patients. JAMA Netw Open. 2020;3(3):e201753. doi: 10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2020.1753 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Walker J, Leveille S, Bell S, et al. OpenNotes after 7 years: patient experiences with ongoing access to their clinicians’ outpatient visit notes. J Med internet Res. 2019;21(5):e13876. doi: 10.2196/13876 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Hibbard JH, Greene J. What the evidence shows about patient activation: better health outcomes and care experiences; fewer data on costs. Health Aff (Millwood). 2013;32(2):207-214. doi: 10.1377/hlthaff.2012.1061 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Henderson C, Nahmias ZP, Fossa A, Barnes E, Huang S. Analysis of patient preferences on patient-provider interactions through the OpenNotes online portal in dermatology. Int J Womens Dermatol. 2021;7(5 part B):793-798. doi: 10.1016/j.ijwd.2021.10.001 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Yanovsky RL, Mostaghimi A, Buzney E, Watson A. Patient ability to interpret dermatopathology reports in an academic dermatology practice. JAMA Dermatol. 2020;156(3):341-342. doi: 10.1001/jamadermatol.2019.4195 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Blease C, Torous J, Hägglund M. Does patient access to clinical notes change documentation? Front Public Health. 2020;8:577896. doi: 10.3389/fpubh.2020.577896 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Supplement 1. eAppendix. Survey Click here for additional data file. (237.4KB, pdf) Supplement 2. Data Sharing Statement Click here for additional data file. (13.1KB, pdf)',57,'2025-10-14 06:53:56','2025-10-14 06:53:56');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2683,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6459093','Sex and Racial/Ethnic Diversity of US Medical Students and Their Exposure to Dermatology Programs.','Barnes LA, Bae GH, Nambudiri VE.','2019','suim suim micrographic redwood suim suim suim suim suim suim suim suim suim steinwachs reese nambudiri pritchett loayza dicaprio shinkai','Abstract This study characterizes sex and racial/ethnic discrepancies in exposure to dermatology among US medical students. Increasing medical workforce diversity improves access to health care in underserved communities. 1 Although sex diversity has increased in dermatology, racial/ethnic diversity lags behind other specialties. 2 , 3 We hypothesized that a lack of dermatology exposure during medical school for students underrepresented in medicine (SUIM) may be contributory and characterized sex and racial/ethnic discrepancies in early dermatology exposure opportunities for medical students. Methods We obtained medical student sex and race/ethnicity enrollment data from the 2017-2018 academic year from the Association of American Medical Colleges. We defined SUIM as individuals reporting black or African American, Hispanic or Latino, Native American, and/or Native Hawaiian or Pacific Islander race/ethnicity. Institutions were cross-referenced for presence of American College of Graduate Medical Education (ACGME)–accredited dermatology residency programs and residency program size. Programs were stratified as small (≤9 residents), medium (10-14 residents), and large (≥15 residents). Institutional presence of ACGME-accredited micrographic surgery and dermatopathology fellowships, American Board of Dermatology–approved pediatric dermatology fellowships, and Dermatology Interest Group Association–verified interest groups were identified to quantify dermatology exposure opportunities. Data were analyzed from May 17 through August 7, 2018. The institutional review boards of Stanford University School of Medicine, Redwood City, California, and Brigham and Women’s Hospital, Boston, Massachusetts, waived the need for approval. Results The ACGME-accredited dermatology residency programs were associated with 102 out of 147 total medical schools, providing potential exposure to 70 954 out of 89 904 US medical students (78.9%). The percentage of female medical students at schools without dermatology residency programs (9109 [48.1%]) was similar to that at schools with small (9656 [49.3%]), medium (13 428 [47.4%]), and large (11 378 [49.4%]) residency programs ( Figure 1 ). The SUIM constituted lower percentages in schools with associated dermatology residencies (9034 [12.7%]) than those without (3180 [16.8%]). The percentages of SUIM were different at schools with small (2665 [13.6%]), medium (3438 [12.1%]), and large (2931 [12.7%]) residency programs. In contrast, Asian medical students constituted 3663 students (19.3%) at institutions without residency programs and steadily increasing percentages at institutions with small (3946 [20.1%]), medium (6040 [21.3%]), and large (5543 [24.1%]) residency programs. Figure 1. Population Comparisons: Program Size. Open in a new tab Sex and racial/ethnic diversity of medical students in cross-referenced dermatology residency program size. SUIM indicates students underrepresented in medicine. The greatest percentage of SUIM was enrolled at schools without additional dermatology exposure opportunities (3143 [17.6%]), whereas a smaller percentage was enrolled at schools with 1 (3528 [14.5%]), 2 (2512 [10.7%]), 3 (1824 [11.7%]), and 4 (1207 [13.6%]) additional exposure opportunities ( Figure 2 ). The lowest percentage of Asian medical students was enrolled at schools without additional exposure opportunities (3318 [18.6%]), whereas the greatest percentage was enrolled at schools with 4 additional exposure opportunities (2296 [25.9%]). No significant differences were noted for female students. Figure 2. Population Comparisons: Exposure Opportunities. Open in a new tab Sex and racial/ethnic diversity of medical students in additional dermatology exposure opportunities, including fellowship programs and dermatology interest groups. SUIM indicates students underrepresented in medicine. Discussion Early exposure and mentorship opportunities are associated with higher rates of students pursuing careers in specific fields. 4 We examined associated dermatology residency programs, fellowships, and interest groups as proxies of opportunities for early dermatology exposure. Medical schools without early exposure opportunities correlated with higher proportions of SUIM. Sex diversity did not vary across groups. Our study is limited because defining dermatology exposure is difficult. The presence of dermatology fellowships may not guarantee significant exposure opportunities; however, we believe that fellowships require institutions to have educational infrastructures supportive of dermatology. Dermatology shadowing programs for students may be better exposure proxies, although more difficult to identify. Nonetheless, given the correlation between higher proportions of SUIM and institutions with limited dermatology exposure opportunities, such schools might benefit from targeted support. Opportunities for mentorship and funding for clinical rotations such as the American Academy of Dermatology diversity mentorship program 5 should be promoted. Dermatology faculty should be encouraged to engage in such mentorship. Partnerships between exposure-rich and exposure-poor schools could be facilitated. Dermatologists should be mindful of continuing mentorship and opportunities for SUIM choosing to pursue dermatology, including programmatic efforts. 6 To promote racial and ethnic diversity within dermatology and ensure the delivery of equitable and effective health care for an increasingly diverse population, further research into barriers limiting pursuit of dermatology careers by SUIM and the development of programs to address such barriers is needed. References 1. Cooper LA, Roter DL, Johnson RL, Ford DE, Steinwachs DM, Powe NR. Patient-centered communication, ratings of care, and concordance of patient and physician race. Ann Intern Med. 2003;139(11):907-915. doi: 10.7326/0003-4819-139-11-200312020-00009 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Bae G, Qiu M, Reese E, Nambudiri V, Huang S. Changes in sex and ethnic diversity in dermatology residents over multiple decades. JAMA Dermatol. 2016;152(1):92-94. doi: 10.1001/jamadermatol.2015.4441 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Pritchett EN, Pandya AG, Ferguson NN, Hu S, Ortega-Loayza AG, Lim HW. Diversity in dermatology: roadmap for improvement. J Am Acad Dermatol. 2018;79(2):337-341. doi: 10.1016/j.jaad.2018.04.003 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Bernstein J, Dicaprio MR, Mehta S. The relationship between required medical school instruction in musculoskeletal medicine and application rates to orthopaedic surgery residency programs. J Bone Joint Surg Am. 2004;86-A(10):2335-2338. doi: 10.2106/00004623-200410000-00031 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. American Academy of Dermatology. Diversity mentorship program: information for medical students. 2018; https://www.aad.org/members/leadership-institute/mentoring/find-a-mentor/diversity-mentorship-program . Accessed August 19, 2018. 6. Chen A, Shinkai K. Important considerations for diversity in the selection of dermatology applicants. JAMA Dermatol. 2017;153(9):949. doi: 10.1001/jamadermatol.2017.1812 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',542,'2025-10-14 06:54:05','2025-10-14 06:54:05');
